{
  // Mesura's Windows configuration operations (architecture section 4.9, vault D023, D024, D028). To change the catalog without
  // a rebuild, put an edited copy in %LOCALAPPDATA%\Mesura (the app, the tray and the CLI all read it there). Risk classes follow the 2026-09-29 reassessment's risk model (section 8):
  // userPreference (R1), systemConfiguration (R2), undocumented (R3), manual, observe (R0).
  // Each "source" says where the setting was taken from; values were read back on PC-000 (Windows 11 26200) where
  // noted. Every applied change records its exact previous value, and 'mesura windows revert' restores it.
  // A registry setting may carry "absentMatches" (the value is Windows' default, so a missing value already counts as
  // done) or "clearBits" (a bit field: only those bits are cleared, every other bit is kept). An operation may carry
  // "requires": noBattery and/or noModernStandby, for changes that only make sense on a desktop, and proEdition for a machine policy
  // Windows Home ignores. "valueBasis" says how the stored value is known to be right: documented (Microsoft documents it), verified
  // (checked read-only on a Windows build: name present in the implementing components and the Settings toggle reads the state the
  // value gives; docs/value-verification-2026-10.md) or community (community sources only); a profile's automatic selection holds
  // only documented and verified entries.
  // The 2026-10-22 catalog audit (repo docs/windows-catalog-audit.md) records why each entry and preset is what it is.
  // Each operation has an "impact" (performance, network, privacy, interface, system or power: what it is about, never
  // how much it helps) and a one-line "summary" for the app's lists. "benefit" (preference, documented, unverified,
  // measured) says what is known about its effect; without it, a user preference claims nothing, an undocumented knob is
  // unverified and documented configuration is documented. "tcp" sets a global TCP/IP setting through netsh
  // (autotuninglevel, rss or rsc only; schema 3, 2026-10-24 quality pass). A preset's "tier" says where the app offers it: "primary"
  // (the two outcomes on Optimize), "specialized" (shown when it applies, such as on a Ghost Spectre image) or
  // "collection" (a quick selection in All settings); its "optional" entries are offered unticked beside it.
  // Translations of names and texts live in the app (Mesura.App\Localization), not here.
  // Schema 2 marks the audited catalog with these fields: a build before it refuses this file rather than misread it,
  // and this build sets aside an edited copy of schema 1 and uses its built-in catalog instead (it says so).
  "kind": "Mesura.WindowsCatalog",
