    {
      "id": "edge.diagnostic-data-required",
      "name": "Send only required diagnostic data from Edge",
      "category": "Microsoft Edge",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Edge sends required diagnostic data only, no optional browsing and crash data.",
      "description": "This sets the Edge policy DiagnosticData to 1 (RequiredData). Required diagnostic data is what Microsoft says keeps Edge secure, up to date and performing as expected. Optional diagnostic data (value 2) includes how you use the browser, websites you visit and crash reports. Setting 1 turns optional collection off while keeping required data. From Edge 122 this policy works on Windows 10 and 11 separately from Windows' own diagnostic data setting.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Edge will show 'Managed by your organization'. Required data is still sent; value 0 (Off) exists but Microsoft marks it 'not recommended' and this entry does not use it. Needs an Edge restart (no dynamic refresh). On Edge 121 and earlier, Windows 10 ignores this policy and follows the Windows diagnostic data setting instead. Without the policy Edge follows the user's own preference. Crash reports no longer reach Microsoft's optional channel, which can make support harder.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn, Edge policy DiagnosticData: Off (0), RequiredData (1) sends required data and 'turns off optional diagnostic data collection', OptionalData (2) includes 'data about browser usage, websites that are visited, crash reports'; REG_DWORD, Windows Edge 122+; 'Can be recommended: No'; restart required. Obsolete predecessors MetricsReportingEnabled and SendSiteInfoToImproveServices stop working after Edge 88.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/diagnosticdata",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/metricsreportingenabled",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/sendsiteinfotoimproveservices"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge",
          "name": "DiagnosticData",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "interface.menu-show-delay",
      "name": "Open submenus faster",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Submenus open after 200 ms of hovering instead of the default 400 ms.",
      "description": "When the pointer rests on a menu item that has a submenu, Windows waits MenuShowDelay milliseconds before it opens the submenu. The Windows default is 400. Mesura sets 200. It changes how soon menus react to the pointer; it does not make any program run faster.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Perceived responsiveness only. It affects menus drawn by Windows' own menu code (classic Win32 menus and apps that follow the system setting); menus drawn by WinUI/XAML, Chromium or other toolkits use their own timing and ignore it. A 2018 reader comment on Winaero reports the value stopped having an effect on Windows 10 build 17134, so treat the effect as unverified on current builds. A lower delay opens submenus when the pointer merely passes over them; 0 makes menus hard to use (Winaero advises against it). The figure 200 comes from community advice (Winaero: start around 200; How-To Geek: 150-200), not from Microsoft, which documents only the value and its meaning. The value is a string; a missing value means 400. Applies after sign-out.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived Windows 2000 resource kit, 'MenuShowDelay'): HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop, REG_SZ, milliseconds in decimal, default 400, 'the interval from the time the cursor is pointed at a menu until the menu items are displayed'. Microsoft Learn SystemParametersInfo: SPI_SETMENUSHOWDELAY 'sets the time, in milliseconds, that the system waits before displaying a shortcut menu when the mouse cursor is over a submenu item'. The chosen 200 ms is community advice (Winaero, How-To Geek).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-2000-server/cc978617(v=technet.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-systemparametersinfoa",
        "https://winaero.com/how-to-speed-up-menus-in-windows-10-and-change-the-menu-delay/",
        "https://www.howtogeek.com/250998/how-to-speed-up-menu-animations-in-windows/"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Control Panel\\Desktop",
          "name": "MenuShowDelay",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "200"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.dynamic-tick-default",
      "name": "Remove the forced constant timer tick",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Deletes the debugging option that keeps the system timer tick running when idle.",
      "description": "The BCD element disabledynamictick set to yes switches off Windows' dynamic timer tick, so the periodic system clock interrupt keeps firing even when nothing needs it. Microsoft documents the element as 'Enables and disables dynamic timer tick feature' and says it should only be used for debugging. This deletes it. Microsoft separately documents that a faster system clock tends to raise power use, and that shortening the timer period from 15.6 ms to 1 ms drains a battery at least 20 percent faster; that figure is about the timer period requested by programs, not about this option, and is quoted only to show the direction of the cost. No performance gain is claimed.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Does nothing on a PC where the element is not set. Needs a restart. Microsoft's page does not state in words that dynamic tick is on by default; this is inferred from the element being a switch that turns the feature off, so treat the 'default' as the absence of the element. Some games or latency tools are reported by communities to behave differently with the tick forced on; that is anecdotal and not measured by Mesura. Edits the boot configuration store (BitLocker or Secure Boot may need suspending on some PCs per Microsoft).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'BCDEdit /set': 'disabledynamictick [yes|no] Enables and disables dynamic timer tick feature. This option should only be used for debugging'; '/deletevalue' returns an option to its default. Microsoft Learn 'Results for the Idle Energy Efficiency Assessment': default system timer 15.6 ms, 1 ms drains the battery at least 20 percent faster; Microsoft Learn 'High-Resolution Timers': faster clock interrupts 'tend to increase power consumption'. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/bcdedit--set",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/bcdedit--set",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/test/assessments/results-for-the-idle-energy-efficiency-assessment",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/kernel/high-resolution-timers"
      ],
      "system": [
        {
          "kind": "bcd",
          "name": "disabledynamictick",
          "value": "absent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.core-parking-off",
      "name": "Keep all processor cores unparked (on AC)",
      "category": "Performance",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Sets the core-parking minimum to 100% on AC so Windows stops parking cores.",
      "description": "Core parking lets Windows put some logical processors into a deep idle ('parked') state when load is low and wake them when load rises. The processor setting 'Processor performance core parking min cores' (powercfg alias CPMINCORES) is the minimum percentage of logical processors per NUMA node that may be unparked at any time. Microsoft documents that at 100 percent the core parking algorithm is disabled. This sets 100 on AC power in the active plan. The setting is hidden (visible only with powercfg /qh). No frame-rate or latency gain is claimed; the cost is the idle power of cores that stay awake.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Desktop-oriented: the entry is skipped on PCs with a battery and changes only the AC value of the active plan. It changes only the main core class. On processors with performance and efficiency cores, the efficiency cores have a separate setting (CPMINCORES1, 'min cores for Processor Power Efficiency Class 1') that this entry does not touch, so those cores can still be parked. Windows' value depends on the plan and the processor: on one Windows 11 PC (PC-000) the Balanced and High performance plans already read 100 on AC (12 percent on battery), so nothing changes there; on other PCs Balanced may park cores. Microsoft's page states no default values. Raises idle power use and heat slightly on PCs that did park cores. Microsoft says silicon vendors tune these settings and recommends consulting them before changing processor power settings. Plan-level changes by the 'Ultimate Performance' or 'High performance' entries may already cover this.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'CPMinCores': 'the minimum percentage of logical processors... that can be placed in the un-parked state at any given time... The Core Parking algorithm is disabled if the value of this setting is 100%'; range 0-100, powercfg alias CPMINCORES, hidden setting. Microsoft Learn 'Processor power management options' (note: silicon vendors tune PPM; contact them before modifying). GUIDs read from powercfg /qh on Windows 11 build 26200: subgroup 54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00 (SUB_PROCESSOR), setting 0cc5b647-c1df-4637-891a-dec35c318583 (Processor performance core parking min cores). https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-core-parking-cpmincores",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-core-parking-cpmincores",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configure-processor-power-management-options"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "0cc5b647-c1df-4637-891a-dec35c318583",
          "name": "Processor performance core parking min cores",
          "ac": 100
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mmcss.network-throttling-default",
      "name": "Restore MMCSS network throttling to its default",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Puts the MMCSS cap on network receive processing back to 10 packets per ms.",
      "description": "While multimedia is playing, MMCSS tells the network stack to pass up at most 10 packets per millisecond (about 10,000 per second, roughly 15 MB/s of full-size frames), so that long network DPCs do not make audio glitch. NetworkThrottlingIndex holds that number: 10 is the default and 0xFFFFFFFF switches the cap off. Tweak packs set 0xFFFFFFFF. This puts 10 back.",
      "risk": "undocumented",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "This undoes a tweak; on a PC where the value is already 10 (it reads 10 on Windows 11 build 26200) nothing changes. Cost of the default: while a multimedia app is active, receive throughput of non-multimedia traffic on a gigabit or faster LAN can be capped near 15 MB/s; this is why some people turn the cap off. Cost of turning it off: a community xperf comparison (djdallmann) reports NDIS.sys DPC latency 'increased quite notably when disabled' and no latency gain for games; no figures are given. The value is not listed on Microsoft's current MMCSS page; the mechanism comes from a Microsoft engineer's 2007 blog post (written for Vista, before a fix was promised), the name, default and 0xFFFFFFFF from a vendor knowledge-base article and community pages. Whether later Windows versions changed the cap internally is not documented. Valid range per the vendor article is 1 to 70 decimal plus 0xFFFFFFFF, which the article itself does not reconcile.",
      "source": "Microsoft (Mark Russinovich, 2007, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/markrussinovich/vista-multimedia-playback-and-network-throughput): MMCSS throttles NDIS to at most 10 packets per millisecond to avoid audio glitching from long network DPCs. TIBCO KB 38041 (https://support.tibco.com/s/article/Tibco-KnowledgeArticle-Article-38041): NetworkThrottlingIndex under HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Multimedia\\SystemProfile, default 10, FFFFFFFF turns throttling off, restart required, applies when time-sensitive multimedia programs run. djdallmann GamingPCSetup network research (https://djdallmann.github.io/GamingPCSetup/CONTENT/RESEARCH/NETWORK): 0xFFFFFFFF off / 10 on (default); disabling worsens NDIS.sys DPC latency. Default 10 also read locally (build 26200).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/markrussinovich/vista-multimedia-playback-and-network-throughput",
        "https://support.tibco.com/s/article/Tibco-KnowledgeArticle-Article-38041",
        "https://djdallmann.github.io/GamingPCSetup/CONTENT/RESEARCH/NETWORK"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Multimedia\\SystemProfile",
          "name": "NetworkThrottlingIndex",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "10"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.nvme-stay-active-ac",
      "name": "Keep NVMe drives out of low-power idle (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "NVMe drives stay in their working state instead of idle power states on AC.",
      "description": "Sets the transition latency tolerance that Windows' in-box NVMe driver (StorNVMe) compares with a drive's entry plus exit latency to 0 ms on AC power, for both the primary and the secondary idle state. With no tolerance the driver finds no idle power state it may enter, so the drive stays in its working state between requests. Microsoft's built-in Performance scheme uses 0 ms on AC for both values; Balanced uses 15 ms (primary) and 100 ms (secondary) on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Active plan only, AC only; battery behaviour isn't touched. Idle power and drive temperature rise a little. No measurement shows faster game loading or lower latency: the only documented change is that the drive no longer enters an idle state it must wake from. Applies to NVMe drives on Windows' in-box driver; Microsoft's page (written for Windows 10) doesn't cover vendor NVMe drivers and doesn't say whether a drive's own autonomous power-state transitions are affected. The Modern Standby row of Microsoft's table is separate and isn't changed. Defaults can differ by build and OEM; the recorded before-image is what gets restored.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'NVMe' (power management for storage devices): GUIDs fc95af4d-... and dbc9e238-... are the primary/secondary latency tolerance in ms, 'the higher this value, the more likely that a deeper power state will be chosen', and the default table gives the Performance scheme 0 ms on AC for both. Values read from powercfg /qh and the DefaultPowerSchemeValues registry keys on a Windows 11 26200 PC (Balanced AC 15/100, High performance AC 0/0).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/design/component-guidelines/power-management-for-storage-hardware-devices-nvme"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "0012ee47-9041-4b5d-9b77-535fba8b1442",
          "setting": "fc95af4d-40e7-4b6d-835a-56d131dbc80e",
          "name": "Primary NVMe power state transition latency tolerance",
          "ac": 0
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "0012ee47-9041-4b5d-9b77-535fba8b1442",
          "setting": "dbc9e238-6de9-49e3-92cd-8c2b4946b472",
          "name": "Secondary NVMe power state transition latency tolerance",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.cpu-epp-performance-ac",
      "name": "Favour speed in the processor energy hint (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Asks the CPU to favour speed over energy saving while on AC power.",
      "description": "Sets the processor energy performance preference (EPP) to 0 on AC power. Per Microsoft, 0 programs the CPU's energy performance preference register to 0, which favours performance; 100 programs 255, which favours energy saving. The setting exists for the main cores and, separately, for 'efficiency class 1' on CPUs with more than one core type; both are set so every core type gets the same hint. Windows' built-in High performance plan uses 0; Balanced uses 33 on AC on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Microsoft documents the register being programmed on CPUs that implement CPPC version 2 with autonomous mode on. On the test PC the plan setting for autonomous mode reads Enabled in all three built-in plans, but Windows ignores that setting on platforms that support only one mode, so whether the value changes anything depends on the CPU, firmware and driver. The Power mode slider (Best performance/Better battery overlays) overrides processor settings of the plan; on the default mode the plan value applies. More heat, fan noise and idle power; battery behaviour isn't touched. No frame-rate or latency gain is documented or measured here. Active plan only, AC only. The class 1 setting may not exist on every PC.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'PerfEnergyPreference': alias PERFEPP/PERFEPP1, 0-100 %, 0 programs the EPP register to 0 'to favor performance', 100 to 255, only on CPPC v2 systems with autonomous mode enabled; 'PerfAutonomousMode'; 'Customize the Windows performance power slider' (overlays, and the advice to copy High performance settings into Balanced). GUIDs and per-plan defaults (Balanced 33, High performance 0, Power saver 60 on AC) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-perfenergypreference",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-perfautonomousmode",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/customize-power-slider"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "36687f9e-e3a5-4dbf-b1dc-15eb381c6863",
          "name": "Processor energy performance preference policy",
          "ac": 0
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "36687f9e-e3a5-4dbf-b1dc-15eb381c6864",
          "name": "Processor energy performance preference policy for efficiency class 1",
