      "minBuild": 22000,
      "limitations": "Microsoft states it doesn't apply to CPUs running in autonomous performance mode, and its page lists Windows 11 only. On the test PC the plan setting for autonomous mode reads Enabled in all three built-in plans, so the value probably does nothing there; it matters only on CPUs whose performance state is chosen by Windows. Higher short-term power draw, heat and fan activity where it does act. No measured responsiveness or frame-time gain is available here. The efficiency-class-1 copy (PERFINCPOL1) is left alone: Windows keeps it at 3 in every built-in plan. Active plan only, AC only.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'PerfIncreasePolicy': alias PERFINCPOL, values 0 ideal / 1 one step closer / 2 'highest speed/power processor performance state' / 3 ideal optimised for responsiveness, not applicable to autonomous performance states, Windows 11 x64. GUID and plan defaults (Balanced 0, High performance 2, Power saver 1) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
