    {
      "id": "visual.transparency-off",
      "name": "Turn off transparency effects",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Makes the taskbar, Start and other surfaces opaque instead of see-through.",
      "description": "Turns off Windows' 'Transparency effects' (the Settings > Personalization > Colors switch, stored per user as EnableTransparency: 1 = on, 0 = off). Surfaces such as the taskbar, Start menu and system flyouts are drawn solid, so the compositor no longer blends and blurs what is behind them. Microsoft's performance guide says Windows 11 visual effects use system resources and that reducing them can improve responsiveness; it does not name transparency specifically.",
