      "limitations": "No measured gain is documented; any saving is small and mostly relevant on weak integrated graphics. Looks flatter. Per-user. Windows default: on (value 1 is present on a stock profile). One source says to restart the PC or sign out for it to apply everywhere. Do not combine with a claim of FPS improvement in games: games drawn in exclusive or flip-model presentation do not use this effect.",
      "source": "Two independent how-tos agree on path, type and meaning: Winaero ('HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Themes\\Personalize', 32-bit DWORD EnableTransparency, 1 = enable, 0 = disable) and NinjaOne (same path, REG_DWORD, 1 = enables, 0 = disables transparency effects for the current user; its PowerShell example has the values reversed, which contradicts its own text). Value read as 1 on Windows 11 build 26200 (default on). Microsoft support 'Tips to improve PC performance in Windows': visual effects can use system resources, 'Adjust for best performance' can improve responsiveness (transparency not named).",
