      "limitations": "Microsoft's own text lists the costs: it only improves apps on the primary display, still-blurry apps remain possible on secondary displays with a different scale, it does not work for all older apps, and 'you may see some odd behavior in some desktop applications' (then it should be disabled). Needs Windows 10 1803 or later and a policy-aware edition (proEdition). The per-user Settings switch 'Let Windows try to fix apps so they're not blurry' is reported to be on by default since Windows 10 1903, so what this policy adds on a current Windows is not established, and neither is how the two interact or whether Windows reads the value from the user or the machine hive. Not a performance setting. Windows default: not configured.",
      "source": "DisplayPerProcessSystemDpiSettings in Display.admx and its Display.adml explanatory text, read from C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions on Windows 11 build 26200: supported on Windows 10 RS4 (1803) and later, class Both; enum element writes decimal 1 (Enable) or 0 (Disable) to value EnablePerProcessSystemDPI under Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Control Panel\\Desktop; text: 'When you enable this policy some blurry applications will be crisp after they are restarted, without requiring the user to log out' plus the caveats quoted above; 'If you disable or do not configure this setting, Per Process System DPI will not apply to any processes on the system'. The value is absent under HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop on the reference PC. No Microsoft Learn page was fetched for this policy.",
