      "id": "network.netbios-over-tcpip-off",
      "name": "Turn off NetBIOS over TCP/IP on every adapter",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Stops legacy NetBIOS name broadcasts and the NBT ports; names resolve via DNS",
      "description": "NetBIOS over TCP/IP (NetBT) is the legacy name service Windows can fall back to when DNS cannot resolve a short host name. Each network interface has a NetbiosOptions value: 0 uses the DHCP server's setting, 1 enables NetBIOS, 2 disables it. This sets NetbiosOptions = 2 on every interface under NetBT\\Parameters\\Interfaces (the same switch as 'Disable NetBIOS over TCP/IP' in the adapter's WINS tab).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Anything that still depends on NetBIOS name lookup stops working on that interface: finding older NAS boxes, printers or PCs by short name without DNS or LLMNR, SMB1-era network browsing and some very old software. Modern file sharing (SMB over port 445), DNS names and IP addresses are unaffected. Security tooling commonly recommends turning NBT-NS off because its broadcast answers can be spoofed on a shared network; that is guidance from security vendors, not a Microsoft statement fetched here. Default on a normal PC is 0 (use the DHCP setting; the reference PC shows 0 on all six interfaces). The archived Windows Server 2003 reference says a change applies after 'ipconfig /renew' on DHCP interfaces or otherwise after a reboot. Interfaces created later (new adapters, VPNs) are not covered until the setting is applied again.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (unattend reference, NetBT NetbiosOptions): 0 = use the DHCP setting if available, 1 = NetBIOS enabled (default when DHCP is not available), 2 = NetBIOS disabled. Microsoft Learn archived 'Appendix B: NetBIOS over TCP/IP Configuration Parameters': NetbiosOptions is a REG_DWORD per interface under NetBT\\Parameters\\Interfaces, changes apply after ipconfig /renew or reboot. Key name form Tcpip_{GUID} confirmed by a read-only look at a Windows 11 PC and by the NetworkingDsc NetBios resource.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/unattend/microsoft-windows-netbt-interfaces-interface-netbiosoptions",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc775874(v=ws.10)",
        "https://www.powershellgallery.com/packages/NetworkingDsc/7.2.0.0/Content/DSCResources%5CMSFT_NetBios%5CMSFT_NetBios.psm1"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\NetBT\\Parameters\\Interfaces\\*",
          "name": "NetbiosOptions",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.wifi-power-saving-max-performance",
      "name": "Wi-Fi adapter power saving: Maximum Performance (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Sets the wireless adapter's power-saving mode to Maximum Performance when plugged in",
      "description": "The Wireless Adapter Settings > Power Saving Mode setting of a power plan tells the Wi-Fi driver how aggressively to save power: 0 Maximum Performance, 1 Low Power Saving, 2 Medium Power Saving, 3 Maximum Power Saving. This sets it to 0 for the AC (plugged in) side of the active plan.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Only the AC value is changed, so a laptop on battery keeps its battery behaviour. Intel states Maximum Performance optimizes performance over power saving and warns that on battery it drains the battery faster; Intel also strongly recommends not changing the default wireless power settings, and says Low and Medium Power Saving behave identically on its adapters. On the reference PC the Balanced plan already has AC = 0 (Maximum Performance) and DC = 2, so on many PCs this changes nothing and is mainly useful where an OEM or custom plan sets the AC value higher. How much real latency or throughput this changes depends on the Wi-Fi driver and is not measured. The GUIDs and index meanings come from this PC's own powercfg output, Intel's article and third-party guides: Microsoft's power-setting reference page for this GUID could not be fetched (404), so the value is not Microsoft-documented. Windows or the OEM may hide the Wireless Adapter Settings entry in the power options UI, but the value can still be read and written with powercfg. Applies to the active plan only; a different plan keeps its own value.",
      "source": "GUIDs and value names read from this Windows 11 PC with powercfg /qh: subgroup 19cbb8fa-5279-450e-9fac-8a3d5fedd0c1 (Wireless Adapter Settings), setting 12bbebe6-58d6-4636-95bb-3217ef867c1a (Power Saving Mode), indexes 0 Maximum Performance, 1 Low Power Saving, 2 Medium Power Saving, 3 Maximum Power Saving; Balanced plan AC = 0, DC = 2. Intel support article on wireless power settings describes the modes. The learn.microsoft.com power-setting page for this GUID returned 404 when fetched, so the GUIDs rest on the OS's own powercfg output plus Intel's description.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/articles/000005879/wireless.html"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "19cbb8fa-5279-450e-9fac-8a3d5fedd0c1",
          "setting": "12bbebe6-58d6-4636-95bb-3217ef867c1a",
          "name": "Wireless adapter power saving mode",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.llmnr-off",
      "name": "Turn off LLMNR",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Stops Windows asking the local subnet to resolve names that DNS could not.",
      "description": "LLMNR (link-local multicast name resolution) is a secondary name resolution protocol. When DNS cannot resolve a short name, Windows can send a multicast query to other PCs on the same subnet and accept any answer. This sets the DNS Client policy 'Turn off multicast name resolution', which Microsoft documents as disabling LLMNR on all available network adapters. DNS is tried first, so ordinary DNS names are not affected.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A hardening choice, not a speed-up; no performance claim is made. Short names that only LLMNR resolves stop working: another PC or NAS on a home network that your router's DNS does not know, reached by its bare name. Windows can still fall back to NetBIOS or mDNS where those are on. Not configured means LLMNR stays on, so Mesura does not treat an absent value as already matching. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Takes effect after a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_DnsClient, Turn_Off_Multicast: 'Turn off multicast name resolution', registry key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient, value EnableMulticast; enabling the policy disables LLMNR on all adapters, disabling or not configuring leaves it enabled; Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 2004+ and Windows 11. Confirmed against the shipped dnsclient.admx on a Windows 11 26200 PC (enabledValue 0). DNS-then-LLMNR-then-NetBT order from the DNS_SmartMultiHomedNameResolution description on the same page.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-dnsclient"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient",
          "name": "EnableMulticast",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.ipv6-6to4-isatap-off",
      "name": "Turn off 6to4 and ISATAP tunnelling",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Removes the 6to4 and ISATAP IPv6-over-IPv4 tunnel interfaces; native IPv6 stays.",
      "description": "6to4 and ISATAP are transition tricks that carry IPv6 inside IPv4. Microsoft's IPv6 guidance says 6to4 is enabled when an interface has a public IPv4 address and then registers its 6to4 address in DNS, and that ISATAP is off by default. This sets the two 'IPv6 Transition Technologies' policies (6to4 State, ISATAP State) to Disabled, which the shipped policy text describes as 6to4 turned off and no ISATAP interfaces present. Native IPv6 and the Teredo setting are left alone. Microsoft lists this Group Policy as one of two documented ways to switch tunnel interfaces off (the other, DisabledComponents 0x01, turns off all tunnel interfaces) and advises against disabling IPv6 itself. RFC 7526 requires unicast 6to4 to be disabled by default in host implementations.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "On most home PCs behind a router (private IPv4 address, no ISATAP router) there is no 6to4 or ISATAP interface to begin with, so nothing visible changes; the value is a guarantee, not a speed-up, and no performance gain is claimed. Microsoft's pages disagree on 6to4's default (KB 929852 says on with a public IPv4 address; the Policy CSP page says the policy default is off), which is why the value is set explicitly. Corporate setups that use ISATAP (DirectAccess, older intranets) need it; do not use there. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_tcpip: 'Set 6to4 State' and 'Set ISATAP State', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition; Disabled = 'No ... interfaces are present' / 6to4 turned off. Value names 6to4_State and ISATAP_State with string values Default/Enabled/Disabled confirmed in the shipped tcpip.admx (Windows 11 26200). Microsoft Learn KB 929852 'Configure IPv6 for advanced users': Group Policy 6to4/ISATAP/Teredo State = Disabled is the documented way to switch off tunnel interfaces; 'ISATAP and Teredo are disabled by default'; we don't recommend disabling IPv6. RFC 7526 section 5: 6to4 must be off by default on hosts.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-tcpip",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/configure-ipv6-in-windows",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7526.html"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition",
          "name": "6to4_State",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "Disabled"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition",
          "name": "ISATAP_State",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "Disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.ssdp-upnp-services-off",
      "name": "Disable SSDP Discovery and UPnP Device Host",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Stops the two UPnP services from ever starting.",
      "description": "SSDP Discovery finds networked devices that use the Simple Service Discovery Protocol (UPnP devices) and announces devices on this PC. UPnP Device Host lets this PC host UPnP devices. Both are Manual-start services that Windows starts only when something asks for them. This sets both to Disabled so nothing can start them. A Microsoft service-hardening table (written for Windows Server 2016 with Desktop Experience) marks both 'OK to disable', and the CIS Windows 11 benchmark lists both as Level 1 'Disabled'.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "A hardening choice, not a speed-up; no performance gain is claimed. Both services are Manual by default, so they cost little while idle, but SSDP Discovery can be running anyway (it was on the Windows 11 reference PC) and this stops it and prevents it starting. The PC then cannot discover SSDP-based devices (UPnP and DLNA devices, some smart TVs and media servers); disabling UPnP Device Host stops any UPnP device hosted on this PC. Secondary reports say Windows then also cannot keep Network discovery turned on; this was not tested. Any service that explicitly depends on either becomes unable to start. Apps that use their own UPnP port-mapping code are not covered by the sources, so no claim is made about them. Needs a restart to guarantee both are stopped.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Security guidelines for system services' table: SSDPSRV and upnphost, startup type Manual, recommendation 'OK to disable'; text 'If you stop this service, the computer can't discover SSDP-based devices' and 'any hosted UPnP devices stop functioning'; (the page is written for Windows Server with Desktop Experience, the same services exist on the client). CIS Windows 11 benchmark section 5 'System Services' lists SSDP Discovery and UPnP Device Host as Disabled (via a WAPT summary page that I fetched; the Tenable CIS item pages returned 404). Service start types read with sc qc on a Windows 11 26200 PC: both DEMAND_START.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-services/security-guidelines-for-disabling-system-services-in-windows-server",
        "https://wapt.tranquil.it/store/en/tis-cis-guide-disable-services"
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "name": "SSDPSRV",
          "start": "disabled"
        },
        {
          "name": "upnphost",
          "start": "disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
