      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge",
          "name": "SleepingTabsEnabled",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.pin-windows-11-25h2",
      "name": "Stay on Windows 11 25H2",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Windows Update keeps servicing 25H2 and doesn't offer the next feature release.",
      "description": "Sets the 'Select the target Feature Update version' policy to Windows 11, 25H2. Windows Update then asks for that product and version on every scan, so you keep getting 25H2's monthly quality updates but are not offered a newer feature release (26H2 and later) until you change or remove the policy. The entry is gated to build 26200 (25H2), so it only applies on a PC that is already on 25H2.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 26200,
      "maxBuild": 26200,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A Group Policy value; Microsoft lists the policy for Pro, Enterprise and Education, so on Home it does not apply. Settings shows 'Some settings are managed by your organization'. Microsoft says that if you do not update the policy before the version reaches end of servicing, the device is moved on automatically once it is 60 days past end of service for its edition; Microsoft's supported-versions page (read 2026-10-29) gives 2027-10-12 as end of updates for 25H2 on Home/Pro. While a target version is set, feature-update deferrals have no effect. You do not get the next release's new features until you remove the policy. A target version older than the installed one, or an invalid string, makes Windows Update offer no feature updates at all (Microsoft), which is why the entry is limited to build 26200. The policy text says entering a target version means accepting the Microsoft Software License Terms for it. The string '25H2' (4 characters at most, per the ADMX) follows Microsoft's version naming; Microsoft's version-info page (aka.ms/WindowsTargetVersioninfo) now redirects to the supported-versions list and does not show the policy strings in a table, so verify on a test PC that Settings > Windows Update keeps reporting 'up to date'. No speed benefit is claimed.",
      "source": "Learn Policy CSP Update: TargetReleaseVersion/ProductVersion are device policies for Pro/Enterprise/Education, 'must be configured together', GP value names under Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate, product string e.g. 'Windows 11'; Windows 11 ADMX shipped on build 26200 (WindowsUpdate.admx): TargetReleaseVersion=1 enables, text elements ProductVersion and TargetReleaseVersionInfo (max 4 characters), help text 'If you enter an invalid value, you will remain on your current version'; Learn waas-wufb-group-policy 'I want to stay on a specific version' (60 days past end of service, deferrals not in effect); Learn supported-versions-windows-client: 25H2 = build 26200, Home/Pro end of updates 2027-10-12, 26H2 = build 26300. Community guides (ElevenForum) use the same three values with strings such as 23H2, 26H1.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-update",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-wufb-group-policy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/release-health/supported-versions-windows-client",
        "https://www.elevenforum.com/t/specify-target-feature-update-version-in-windows-11.3811/ (search result summary only; page returned 403)"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "TargetReleaseVersion",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "ProductVersion",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "Windows 11"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "TargetReleaseVersionInfo",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "25H2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
