        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A Group Policy value; Microsoft lists it for Pro, Enterprise, Education (Windows 10 1809 and later), so on Home it does not apply. Microsoft warns that if you also stop updates from installing automatically, neither you nor other users will see that important updates are waiting; with the default automatic installation that risk is small, but you will no longer be told about updates or about installs that failed. Level 2 (also hides restart warnings) is not used: Microsoft calls it a poor experience for personal devices. On Windows 11 22H2 and later the separate 'apply only during active hours' option (NoUpdateNotificationsDuringActiveHours) is left unset, so notifications are off at all hours. A notification can still appear after a configured compliance deadline.",
      "source": "Learn Policy CSP Update, UpdateNotificationLevel: 0 (default) default notifications, 1 turn off all notifications excluding restart warnings, 2 including restart warnings; Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 1809 and later. Registry names read from the Windows 11 ADMX on build 26200 (WindowsUpdate.admx): enabling value SetUpdateNotificationLevel=1 plus enum UpdateNotificationLevel (0/1/2) and boolean NoUpdateNotificationsDuringActiveHours. Learn waas-wufb-group-policy: 'Option 2 creates a poor experience for personal devices'. The Learn CSP allowed-values table in one place lists only 0/1; the description and the ADMX agree on 0/1/2.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-update",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-restart",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-wufb-group-policy"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "SetUpdateNotificationLevel",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "UpdateNotificationLevel",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.no-windows-update-driver-search",
      "name": "Don't search Windows Update when installing a device driver",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Windows installs a device from local drivers only, never fetching one from Windows Update.",
      "description": "Sets 'Specify search order for device driver source locations' to 'Do not search Windows Update'. When a device is installed or its driver is reinstalled, Windows looks only in its local driver store and the usual local sources and does not check Windows Update at that moment. It governs the search at device installation only; drivers that Windows Update offers during its own update scans are controlled by the separate entry 'Don't let Windows Update replace drivers'. Meant for a PC where you install drivers yourself.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 19041,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "New hardware Windows has no built-in driver for (network adapters on a fresh install, chipset, Bluetooth, printers, USB devices) will not get a driver from Windows Update until you install it yourself, and a PC with no working network driver can be left without internet. Existing drivers are not removed. Microsoft documents only the search order; it does not say this stops Windows Update replacing a driver you installed, so do not rely on it for that, and use the other driver entry as well. A Group Policy value; Microsoft lists it for Pro, Enterprise and Education (Windows 10 2004 with KB5005101, and Windows 11), so on Home it does not apply. When it is not set, administrators choose the order themselves (Microsoft). Keep the vendor installers for the network adapter at hand. Not for laptops or OEM PCs that rely on Windows Update for drivers.",
      "source": "Learn Policy CSP ADMX_DeviceSetup, DriverSearchPlaces_SearchOrderConfiguration: 'select whether Windows searches for drivers on Windows Update unconditionally, only if necessary, or not at all'; key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DriverSearching; Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 2004 with KB5005101 and Windows 11 21H2 and later. Windows 11 build 26200 DeviceSetup.admx: enum SearchOrderConfig 1 = always search, 2 = only if needed, 0 = do not search Windows Update; help text 'If you disable or do not configure this policy setting, members of the Administrators group can determine the priority order'.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-devicesetup"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DriverSearching",
          "name": "SearchOrderConfig",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "services.link-tracking-off",
      "name": "Turn off Distributed Link Tracking",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Stops the Distributed Link Tracking Client service (TrkWks) from starting.",
      "description": "The Distributed Link Tracking Client searches for files that a shell shortcut or OLE link points to after the file has moved to another NTFS volume or computer, and reports such moves to a domain controller. On a standalone PC with no domain it has little to do. This entry sets the service to Disabled. Microsoft's Windows IoT Enterprise guide (written for fixed-function devices) lists it as 'OK to disable' for configurations that do not use it; Microsoft's Windows Server 2016 guide gives no guidance for it.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Windows default is Automatic (Windows 11 Pro clean-install lists, and read on a build 26200 PC). The service has no trigger start (no TriggerInfo key on that PC); whether anything starts it on demand when set to Manual was not checked, so Disabled is used because it is the value Microsoft's IoT guide names. Cost: shortcuts and OLE links to a file moved to another NTFS volume or machine may no longer be repaired automatically. Domain-joined PCs with roaming shortcuts or Office linked documents should keep it. The memory and CPU saved were not measured; the service is normally idle.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Guidance on configuring system services - Windows IoT Enterprise' (client Windows 10/11, updated 2026-06) lists Distributed Link Tracking Client (TrkWks) as 'OK to disable'; Microsoft Learn Win32 'Distributed Link Tracking and Object Identifiers' describes what the client does (tracks shell-shortcut and OLE link sources on NTFS, searches for moved sources); default Automatic confirmed by smartpcutilities.com Windows 11 Pro defaults list and by reading the service key on a Windows 11 build 26200 PC. Microsoft's Server 2016 guide gives 'No guidance' for it. https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/distributed-link-tracking-and-object-identifiers https://www.smartpcutilities.com/windows-11-default-services-configuration.html",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/distributed-link-tracking-and-object-identifiers",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-services/security-guidelines-for-disabling-system-services-in-windows-server",
        "https://www.smartpcutilities.com/windows-11-default-services-configuration.html"
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "name": "TrkWks",
          "start": "disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.app-shutdown-wait-5s",
      "name": "Shorten the app wait at shutdown to 5 seconds",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Windows waits 5 s, not the default 20 s, for apps to close at sign-out or shutdown.",
      "description": "WaitToKillAppTimeout is how long Windows waits for user programs to end after you sign out or shut down; when it expires the 'did not respond' dialog appears (or the process is ended if AutoEndTasks is 1). Microsoft's default is 20000 ms. Mesura sets 5000 ms.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Only helps when an app is slow to close; a clean shutdown is not faster. A program that needs more than 5 seconds to save its data is flagged as not responding sooner, and with AutoEndTasks=1 it would be ended after 5 seconds, so unsaved data can be lost. The default is from the Windows 2000/2003-era reference; Windows 10/11 community guides give the same default (20000, with HungAppTimeout 5000), but Microsoft does not document the modern shutdown screen, so the visible effect is unverified. 5000 is community advice (Dave's Computer Tips calls it the safer choice; 2000 is described as a floor), not a Microsoft figure. Type is a string. The value is usually absent by default. Applies at the next sign-out.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived Windows 2000 resource kit, 'WaitToKillAppTimeout'): HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop, REG_SZ, milliseconds in decimal, default 20000; 'how long the system waits for user processes to end after the user attempts to log off or to shut down... the End Task dialog box appears'. Related 'HungAppTimeout' page: default 5000. Dave's Computer Tips (Windows 10) gives the same defaults and suggests 5000 as the safer value and warns about data loss.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-2000-server/cc978624(v=technet.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-2000-server/cc978614(v=technet.10)",
        "https://davescomputertips.com/windows-10-quick-tips-app-kill-timeouts"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Control Panel\\Desktop",
          "name": "WaitToKillAppTimeout",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "5000"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "explorer.icons-only-no-thumbnails",
      "name": "Show icons instead of thumbnails in File Explorer",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "File Explorer shows generic icons, never picture or video thumbnails.",
      "description": "Turns on the File Explorer option 'Always show icons, never thumbnails'. Explorer then stops generating and reading previews of images, videos and documents when you open a folder and shows each file's type icon instead.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "explorerRestart",
      "limitations": "You lose previews in File Explorer, file pickers and the desktop for pictures, videos and PDFs. The saving is on folders with many media files, where Explorer has to read the files to build thumbnails; the effect is not measured in any source I found (MajorGeeks only says other visual effects may speed up a computer). It does not affect the taskbar preview or the preview pane. This is the per-user preference; Microsoft's separate Group Policy DisableThumbnails (HKCU\\...\\Policies\\Explorer) enforces the same thing and locks the option, which Mesura does not use. Missing value or 0 = thumbnails shown. Takes effect after Explorer restarts.",
      "source": "MajorGeeks: HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\Advanced, IconsOnly 0 = show thumbnails, 1 = hide thumbnails. The PowerShell Gallery 'VisualEffects' module maps ShowThumbnails to IconsOnly with Off = 1, On = 0. Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_Thumbnails documents the policy equivalent DisableThumbnails ('File Explorer displays only icons and never displays thumbnail images'; thumbnails are shown by default). Two other outlets (Winbuzzer, Ten Forums per search summary) were found with the values reversed or garbled and were not relied on.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.majorgeeks.com/content/page/how_to_disable_or_enable_thumbnail_previews_in_file_explorer.html",
        "https://www.powershellgallery.com/packages/VisualEffects/1.0.6/Content/Definitions%5CRegistry.json",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-thumbnails"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\Advanced",
          "name": "IconsOnly",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "explorer.folder-type-general",
      "name": "Stop File Explorer guessing the folder type",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "New folders use the General view instead of a type chosen by scanning their files.",
      "description": "Explorer normally looks inside a folder and picks a view template (Documents, Pictures, Music, Videos) from the files it finds, which can show a 'Working on it' bar in folders with many files. Setting the default folder type to NotSpecified makes Explorer treat folders as General items and skip that content scan.",
      "risk": "undocumented",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "explorerRestart",
      "limitations": "Folders you have not customised lose their automatic media columns and view (a Pictures or Music folder opens in the generic view, often Details). Folders whose type was already assigned earlier keep it, so the effect shows on folders Explorer has not seen yet; clearing the saved folder views would need a registry-key deletion that Mesura's primitives do not have. The benefit is reported for folders with hundreds or thousands of files (Windows Latest) and is unmeasured; it is small for folders with few files. No Microsoft page documents it; the value has been known since Windows Vista/7 per Windows Latest and Pureinfotech. Missing value = automatic discovery. Takes effect after Explorer restarts or you restart the PC.",
      "source": "Windows Latest (2024-03-04), BGR and Pureinfotech all give HKCU\\Software\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\Bags\\AllFolders\\Shell, string FolderType = NotSpecified, explain it disables automatic folder-type discovery (Explorer otherwise scans files to choose a template) and name the cost as a generic view for all folders; revert by deleting the value. Windows Latest says it helps folders with hundreds or thousands of files and gives no timings.",
      "sources": [
        "https://windowslatest.com/2024/03/04/windows-11-can-open-big-folders-in-file-explorer-faster-if-you-turn-off-folder-discovery",
        "https://bgr.com/2150727/how-to-fix-windows-file-explorer-performance/",
        "https://pureinfotech.com/?p=165204"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\Bags\\AllFolders\\Shell",
          "name": "FolderType",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "NotSpecified"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.user-presence-qos-off",
      "name": "Don't lower the foreground app's QoS when idle",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Stops Windows lowering the foreground app's QoS on battery after you stop typing.",
      "description": "By default Windows may lower the Quality of Service of the foreground app to Medium after a period with no user input, which only matters on battery and lets the CPU run more efficiently. Microsoft documents the registry value DisableUserPresenceQos under HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Power\\PowerThrottling: 1 disables the feature, 0 leaves it enabled. Microsoft recommends disabling it when benchmarking on battery, because tests with no user input can trigger it and skew results.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Documented as affecting battery operation only ('lowering the foreground application after a period of user inactivity (on battery only)'), so a desktop or a plugged-in laptop sees no change. On battery it costs battery life. Microsoft's page marks the inactivity feature as part of release 'Ge 2025.05' and gives no Windows build number, so no build range is set here; on Windows versions without the feature the value is ignored. Microsoft does not say whether a restart is needed; a restart is assumed. No performance gain is claimed beyond avoiding the documented lowering.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Quality of Service' (updated 2025-12): 'By default, Windows may lower the QoS policy of a foreground application to Medium QoS after a period of user inactivity'; 'You can disable this feature by setting... HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Power\\PowerThrottling, DisableUserPresenceQos, REG_DWORD, 1 (feature is disabled), 0 (feature is enabled)'; 'should be disabled when testing on battery'; Medium QoS row: 'lowering the QoS of the foreground application after a period of user inactivity (on battery only)'. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/quality-of-service",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/quality-of-service"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Power\\PowerThrottling",
          "name": "DisableUserPresenceQos",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.hybrid-prefer-performance-cores",
      "name": "Prefer performance cores for all threads on hybrid CPUs",
      "category": "Performance",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Sets the heterogeneous thread scheduling policy to 'Prefer performant processors'.",
      "description": "On CPUs with performance and efficiency cores, Windows 11 chooses a core class per thread from its Quality of Service level. Two hidden power settings override the preference for long-running threads (SCHEDPOLICY) and short-running threads (SHORTSCHEDPOLICY): 0 all, 1 performant only, 2 prefer performant, 3 efficient only, 4 prefer efficient, 5 automatic (Windows decides from the system and QoS). This sets both to 2 on AC power in the active plan.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 22000,
      "limitations": "Only meaningful on hybrid CPUs (for example Intel 12th gen and later, some Arm parts); on other CPUs the setting exists but changes nothing (on a 4-core non-hybrid Core i7 it reads 5, Automatic, in the Balanced plan on build 26200). Microsoft does not say that Automatic is the default and documents no benefit. Microsoft does not describe what Windows does by default for foreground threads; the likely effect is that low-priority and background threads are also pushed onto performance cores, where they can compete with a game for the same cores and draw more power. Hidden settings (Microsoft's page: 'Hidden setting: Yes'); AC value only, battery behaviour is unchanged. Mesura cannot yet restrict an entry to hybrid CPUs. Benefit is unmeasured.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn SchedulingPolicy (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-schedulingpolicy) and ShortSchedulingPolicy (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-shortschedulingpolicy): aliases SCHEDPOLICY and SHORTSCHEDPOLICY, hidden, values 0-5 as listed, Automatic lets the OS decide; Windows 11 supported on x64 and Arm. GUIDs read with powercfg /qh on Windows 11 build 26200: SUB_PROCESSOR 54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00, SCHEDPOLICY 93b8b6dc-0698-4d1c-9ee4-0644e900c85d, SHORTSCHEDPOLICY bae08b81-2d5e-4688-ad6a-13243356654b.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-schedulingpolicy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-shortschedulingpolicy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configure-processor-power-management-options"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "93b8b6dc-0698-4d1c-9ee4-0644e900c85d",
          "name": "Heterogeneous thread scheduling policy",
          "ac": 2
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "bae08b81-2d5e-4688-ad6a-13243356654b",
          "name": "Heterogeneous short running thread scheduling policy",
          "ac": 2
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.cpu-min-state-full-ac",
      "name": "Keep the processor at full performance level (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Minimum processor state 100% on AC: the CPU never drops below full speed.",
      "description": "Sets the minimum processor state to 100% of maximum processor performance on AC power, for both the main setting and the efficiency-class-1 copy. Microsoft defines this setting as the lowest processor performance state, as a percentage of maximum. The built-in High performance plan uses 100 on AC; Balanced uses 5 on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC. How a given CPU maps 100% to a clock speed (base or boost) depends on the CPU and firmware.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "The cost is the largest of the processor settings here: the CPU stops idling down, so power, heat and fan noise rise even at the desktop, and on a laptop the fan may run constantly while plugged in. Desktops with good cooling are the sensible target. No measurement shows a frame-rate gain on current processors. Idle states (C-states) still work; this isn't the same as turning idle off. Active plan only, AC only; the Power mode slider can override parts of the processor policy.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'MinPerformance': alias PROCTHROTTLEMIN/PROCTHROTTLEMIN1, 'minimum processor performance state, as a percentage of maximum processor performance', 0-100, not hidden. GUIDs and plan defaults (Balanced 5, High performance 100, Power saver 5 on AC, both classes) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-minperformance",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/customize-power-slider"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "893dee8e-2bef-41e0-89c6-b55d0929964c",
          "name": "Minimum processor state",
          "ac": 100
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "893dee8e-2bef-41e0-89c6-b55d0929964d",
          "name": "Minimum processor state for efficiency class 1",
          "ac": 100
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.cpu-idle-states-off-ac",
      "name": "Stop the processor entering idle states (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "The CPU spins instead of idling on AC: constant power draw and heat.",
      "description": "Sets 'Processor idle disable' to 1 (Disable idle) on AC power. Microsoft describes the setting this way: if idle states are disabled, the kernel spins in a loop when there is no code to run on a processor instead of moving it to an idle state. The Windows default is 0 (idle enabled) in every built-in plan.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "high",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "A heavy setting for a latency-driven use such as a real-time audio rig, not for general gaming. Every core runs flat out: Task Manager shows near-100% CPU use, idle power and heat jump a lot, fans spin up, and a shared power or thermal limit can leave less headroom for boost. No measurement of a latency or frame-time benefit is available here, and Microsoft's page is an archived Windows 10 reference. Desktops only (a laptop on AC would run hot). Active plan only, AC only; set it back if the PC gets hot or loud.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived) 'IDLEDISABLE': GUID 5d76a2ca-e8c0-402f-a133-2158492d58ad, alias IDLEDISABLE, hidden, 0 Enabled / 1 Disabled, kernel spins in a loop instead of invoking an idle transition. GUID and defaults (0 in Balanced, High performance, Power saver) confirmed from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/mt422913(v=vs.85)"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "5d76a2ca-e8c0-402f-a133-2158492d58ad",
          "name": "Processor idle disable",
          "ac": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.cpu-boost-off-ac",
      "name": "Turn off processor boost (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "The CPU no longer boosts above its base speed on AC: cooler, quieter, slower.",
      "description": "Sets 'Processor performance boost mode' to 0 (Disabled) on AC power, so Windows doesn't let the CPU select performance levels above the nominal level. Microsoft's table lists 0 Disabled and 1 Enabled for all three processor types (ACPI P-state, CPPC/PEP, autonomous CPPC/PEP); 2 means Aggressive only on CPPC/PEP and Enabled on the other two. On the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC all three built-in plans use 2.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "This lowers peak performance: single-thread and short-burst speed fall to the base level, which can cost frame rate in CPU-limited games. It's for people who want less heat, noise or power, not for speed. Not every CPU exposes the same modes. Active plan only, AC only; battery behaviour isn't touched.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'PERFBOOSTMODE': GUID be337238-0d82-4146-a960-4f3749d470c7, alias PERFBOOSTMODE, hidden, 'how processors select a performance level when current operating conditions allow for boosting performance above the nominal level', index 0 = Disabled on all three interface types. Plan defaults (2 in Balanced, High performance, Power saver) read from DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-perfboostmode"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "be337238-0d82-4146-a960-4f3749d470c7",
          "name": "Processor performance boost mode",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.sata-link-power-off-ac",
      "name": "Turn off SATA link power saving (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "SATA drives on AHCI keep their link active instead of powering it down on AC.",
      "description": "Sets the AHCI link power management mode to Active (0) on AC power: Microsoft defines this as link power management not being used, so the SATA link isn't put into a low-power state by the host (HIPM) or the device (DIPM). Balanced uses HIPM (1) on AC on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC; High performance uses 0.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Only for SATA drives behind an AHCI controller; NVMe drives are not affected. The saving at stake is small and so is any benefit: the link doesn't have to wake after idle. No measurement of a speed gain is available. Microsoft's page lists three values (0 Active, 1 HIPM, 2 HIPM and DIPM) while Windows' powercfg also shows DIPM and Lowest; only 0 is used. Active plan only, AC only; some SATA SSD/HDD controllers need link power management to reach deep idle, so a laptop may use more idle power.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Link power management mode - HIPM/DIPM': GUID 0b2d69d7-a2a1-449c-9680-f91c70521c60, hidden, 0 Active ('Link power management is not used'), 1 HIPM, 2 HIPM and DIPM; Windows 7 and later. Plan defaults (Balanced 1, High performance 0, Power saver 2 on AC) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/disk-settings-link-power-management-mode---hipm-dipm"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "0012ee47-9041-4b5d-9b77-535fba8b1442",
          "setting": "0b2d69d7-a2a1-449c-9680-f91c70521c60",
          "name": "AHCI link power management - HIPM/DIPM",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.wake-timers-off-ac",
      "name": "Stop wake timers waking the PC (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Scheduled timers can't wake the PC from sleep while plugged in.",
      "description": "Sets 'Allow wake timers' to Disable (0) on AC power. Microsoft defines the setting as whether the system uses the system-wide wake-on-timer capability to perform scheduled tasks, for example waking automatically to install updates: 0 No, 1 Yes, 2 Important (internal system timers only). On the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC all three built-in plans use 1 on AC and 0 on battery.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "A preference, not a performance setting. Tasks that ask to wake the PC (maintenance, scheduled backups, update installs, alarms) no longer do it and run when the PC is next awake. The setting doesn't affect Wake-on-LAN or waking by key press, power button or lid. Value 2 (Important) is the middle ground if some system timers must still work. Only the AC value is changed; battery already defaults to 0. Active plan only.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Automatically wake for tasks': GUID bd3b718a-0680-4d9d-8ab2-e1d2b4ac806d, alias RTCWAKE, hidden, 0 No (wake on timer disabled), 1 Yes, 2 Important (internal system timers only). Plan defaults (AC 1, DC 0 in all three built-in plans) read from DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/sleep-settings-automatically-wake-for-tasks"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "238c9fa8-0aad-41ed-83f4-97be242c8f20",
          "setting": "bd3b718a-0680-4d9d-8ab2-e1d2b4ac806d",
          "name": "Allow wake timers",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "memory.keep-kernel-in-ram",
      "name": "Disable paging of kernel code and drivers",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Keeps kernel and driver code in RAM instead of letting Windows page it to disk.",
      "description": "Sets DisablePagingExecutive to 1. By default (0) Windows may write kernel-mode drivers and system code to the page file when they are idle and read them back when needed. With 1 that code stays in physical memory. Microsoft's reference says the benefit is the avoided disk reads and that it applies to machines with a lot of memory. A restart is required.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Pinned code is memory programs and the file cache can no longer use, so on a PC with little RAM this can make paging of your programs worse, not better. Microsoft's own text hedges the gain ('this would depend on the installed applications and their resource utilization') and the references are for Windows Server 2003 and a 2007 blog post; neither measures Windows 10/11, and Mesura has not measured it either. No frame-rate or latency gain is claimed. Windows default is 0 (the value is normally absent). Use only on a desktop with plenty of free RAM.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived TechNet, Windows Server 2003 registry reference) 'DisablePagingExecutive': REG_DWORD 0|1, default 0; 0 = drivers and system code can be paged to disk as needed, 1 = they must remain in physical memory; restart required; 'improves performance on machines with a lot of memory, because it prevents disk reads'. Microsoft archived blog SYSK 318 (2007): same values and default 0, 'on machines with a lot of memory, this setting can improve performance, but this would depend on the installed applications and their resource utilization'. No Windows 10/11 page or measurement found (searched).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc757875(v=ws.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/irenak/sysk-318-registry-key-that-controls-device-driver-and-system-code-pagination-to-disk"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\Memory Management",
          "name": "DisablePagingExecutive",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "memory.ntfs-pool-memory",
      "name": "Increase NTFS memory cache limits",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Lets NTFS keep larger paged and non-paged pool caches for file operations.",
      "description": "Sets NtfsMemoryUsage to 2 (the setting behind 'fsutil behavior set memoryusage 2'). With the default (Microsoft's page names 1; Windows also treats 0 as default, which is what fsutil reports on some PCs) NTFS uses its default amount of paged-pool memory; with 2 it enlarges its lookaside lists and memory thresholds. Microsoft says this might improve performance if the system opens and closes many files in the same file set and is not already using large amounts of memory for apps or cache. A restart is required.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Microsoft also states the cost: if the PC already uses a lot of memory for apps or cache, raising the limit reduces the pool memory left for other processes and might reduce overall performance. The benefit is for workloads that open and close many files in the same set (builds, servers, large file trees); games are not such a workload and no gain is claimed or measured. Use on a PC with ample RAM only. The default is 1 (Microsoft) or 0 (fsutil reports 0 as 'default memory consumption values'); the value may be absent or already present as 0 or 1, and the recorded before-image puts back whichever it was.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'fsutil behavior': memoryusage <1|2>, 'When set to 1 (the default), NTFS uses the default amount of paged-pool memory. When set to 2, NTFS increases the size of its lookaside lists and memory thresholds'; the parameter 'updates the HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem\\NtfsMemoryUsage registry key'; 'might improve performance if your system is opening and closing many files in the same file set and is not already using large amounts of system memory'; otherwise 'might reduce overall system performance'; restart required. Another tweak tool's script for 'Configure Memory Usage Behaviors' was not available, so the match is by name only.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/fsutil-behavior"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem",
          "name": "NtfsMemoryUsage",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "memory.clear-pagefile-at-shutdown",
      "name": "Clear the page file at shutdown",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Windows overwrites unused page-file contents when it shuts down; shutdown gets slower.",
      "description": "Sets ClearPageFileAtShutdown to 1. Windows then fills inactive page-file pages with zeros when the system stops, so data from process memory that was paged out is not left readable by someone who removes the drive and reads the page file offline. Active pages cannot be zeroed. This is a privacy setting, not a performance setting; it costs shutdown time.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Slower shutdown and restart, in proportion to the page-file size. Microsoft's example is a server with 2 GB of RAM and a 2 GB page file where it 'could increase the shutdown process by more than 30 minutes'; on a modern SSD the delay is smaller, but Mesura has not measured it. Microsoft notes that an attacker with physical access can bypass it by cutting the power, and its 2003 reference cautions that changing the value 'can degrade the performance of your computer'. Windows' own policy page says the policy also clears hiberfil.sys when hibernation is off on a portable PC. Microsoft does not say whether the page file is cleared on a Fast Startup (hybrid) shutdown, and Mesura has not verified it, so do not rely on it unless Fast Startup is off or you use Restart. Not verified on Windows Home. Default is 0 (disabled).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived TechNet, Windows Server 2003 registry reference) 'ClearPageFileAtShutdown': REG_DWORD 0|1, default 0; 1 = inactive pages are filled with zeros when the system stops; 'Changing this value can degrade the performance of your computer'. Microsoft Learn 'Shutdown: Clear virtual memory pagefile' (Windows 10/11): default Disabled on clients, no restart required, can add a long shutdown delay (2 GB RAM/2 GB pagefile example: more than 30 minutes), bypassed by unplugging power.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc757862(v=ws.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/shutdown-clear-virtual-memory-pagefile"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\Memory Management",
          "name": "ClearPageFileAtShutdown",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1",
          "absentMatches": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "memory.encrypt-pagefile",
      "name": "Encrypt the page file",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "NTFS encrypts the page file; adds processing work to every paging operation.",
      "description": "Sets NtfsEncryptPagingFile to 1 (what 'fsutil behavior set encryptpagingfile 1' does). Microsoft says encrypting the page file prevents malicious users from reading data that has been paged to disk, and that it adds processing overhead for filesystem operations. A restart is required. This is a privacy setting, not a performance setting.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 19041,
      "limitations": "Costs CPU work on every page-file read and write; Microsoft gives no figure, one community guide calls the slowdown small on modern PCs, and Mesura has not measured it. It is largely redundant if the whole drive is already encrypted with BitLocker. Windows default is off. The value name NtfsEncryptPagingFile and 'data 1 = on' come from Microsoft's own policy definition (FileSys.admx, 'Enable NTFS pagefile encryption'); the location HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem is what 'fsutil behavior set encryptpagingfile' uses, which Microsoft does not state on a page, so the value basis stays 'community'. A Group Policy of the same name under ...\\Control\\Policies may also apply; Mesura has not verified which wins. Evidenced for Windows 10 2004 and later; not verified on Windows Home.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'ADMX_FileSys Policy CSP' EnablePagefileEncryption ('Encrypting the page file prevents malicious users from reading data that has been paged to disk, but also adds processing overhead for filesystem operations'; policy value NtfsEncryptPagingFile; Windows 10 2004 with KB5005101 and later, Windows 11) and 'fsutil behavior' (encryptpagingfile 1 encrypts the paging file; restart required). Value data 1/0 and the Control\\FileSystem location: geekrewind.com guide (also notes the extra work per paging-file access is small on most modern PCs); a second guide (elevenforum.com) could not be fetched (HTTP 403), so the data value rests on one community source plus Microsoft's fsutil semantics.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-filesys",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/fsutil-behavior",
        "https://geekrewind.com/how-to-turn-on-or-off-paging-file-encryption-in-windows-11/"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem",
          "name": "NtfsEncryptPagingFile",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.ntfs-8dot3-off",
      "name": "Stop creating 8.3 short file names",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "NTFS stops making a second, DOS-style name for new files.",
      "description": "By default NTFS can keep a second 8.3-style name (like PROGRA~1) next to each long file name, and must look those names up when files are created in a folder. Setting NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation to 1 turns the creation of new short names off for all volumes. Names that already exist are kept.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Windows 10/11 default is 2 (decided per volume; the system volume normally keeps short names). Programs or old installers that look up a short name for a file created after the change can fail. Existing short names are not removed, and Mesura does not strip them: Microsoft warns that removing them can break applications, including uninstalling. Microsoft recommends 1 for SMB file servers; for a desktop PC it gives no figure and Mesura has not measured any gain. Per-volume control (fsutil 8dot3name set D: 1) is not expressible here.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'fsutil behavior': when disable8dot3 is 0 NTFS creates a second entry for every long name and 'must look up' the 8.3 names when creating files; the parameter writes HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem\\NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation. Microsoft Learn 'fsutil 8dot3name': 0 enables for all volumes, 1 disables for all volumes, 2 per volume, 3 disables for all but the system volume; warning that removing names may break applications. Microsoft Learn FSCT page: default is 2 on Windows Server 2012/R2, system volume enabled and others disabled, recommended 1 for most SMB file servers. Value 2 read on a Windows 11 Pro machine.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/fsutil-behavior",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/fsutil-8dot3name",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/dn567658(v=vs.85)"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem",
          "name": "NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.storage-sense-off",
      "name": "Turn off Storage Sense (policy)",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Stops Windows from cleaning files automatically and locks the switch off.",
      "description": "Storage Sense can delete temporary files and old Recycle Bin items on its own (and clean Downloads or cloud-synced files if those thresholds are set). Windows turns it on by itself only when disk space runs low. The policy AllowStorageSenseGlobal = 0 turns it off for the machine and stops users switching it back on in Settings.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 18362,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "You lose the automatic clean-up that would run when free space gets low, so a full system drive stays full until you clear it yourself. With the policy set, Settings greys out the switch. Windows Home ignores machine policies. Without the policy, Microsoft says Storage Sense is off until the user runs into low disk space or enables it, so this is only needed if you want it blocked. It is a preference: no speed claim.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Policy CSP - Storage', AllowStorageSenseGlobal: Disabled = 'Storage Sense is turned off the machine. Users can't enable Storage Sense'; Not Configured = 'turned off until the user runs into low disk space or the user enables it manually'; values 1 allow, 0 block; ADMX registry key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\StorageSense, value AllowStorageSenseGlobal (StorageSense.admx); Pro, Enterprise, Education; Windows 10 1903 and later.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-storage"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\StorageSense",
          "name": "AllowStorageSenseGlobal",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.disk-health-model-off",
      "name": "Stop downloading disk failure-prediction model updates",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Windows stops fetching updates to its disk-failure prediction model.",
      "description": "Windows downloads updated machine-learning model parameters it uses to predict storage-disk failure. The policy AllowDiskHealthModelUpdates = 0 stops those downloads; the model already on the PC stays as it is.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 16299,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "The failure-prediction model stops getting improvements, so disk health warnings may become less accurate over time. The downloads are small and infrequent; this is a preference, not a performance setting. Windows Home ignores machine policies. Microsoft does not say how large or how often the downloads are.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Policy CSP - Storage', AllowDiskHealthModelUpdates: 'Allows downloading new updates to ML Model parameters for predicting storage disk failure'; 0 don't allow, 1 allow (default); Not configured = same as Enabled; ADMX key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\StorageHealth, value AllowDiskHealthModelUpdates (StorageHealth.admx); Pro, Enterprise, Education; Windows 10 1709 and later.",
      "sources": [
