          "name": "AllowDiskHealthModelUpdates",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "visual.show-window-contents-off",
      "name": "Show only an outline while dragging windows",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Dragging or resizing a window moves an outline, not the whole window.",
      "description": "Turns off 'Show window contents while dragging' (the per-user DragFullWindows string). With 0, only the outline of a window moves while you drag it; with 1 (the default), the entire window and its contents move. Microsoft's Windows Server 2003 reference documents the value, its two meanings and its default.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Documented by Microsoft only in the archived Windows Server 2003 reference; the modern-Windows behaviour rests on a how-to site and on the value being present and 1 on Windows 11 build 26200. On current Windows with DWM composition the gain, if any, is unmeasured and likely nil on a PC with a GPU; it matters mostly on very weak or remote-desktop sessions. Dragging looks worse. The same switch also lives in Performance Options (SystemPropertiesPerformance), so the visual-effects dialog can write it back. Takes effect after sign-out, or an Explorer restart per one source.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived Windows Server 2003 Core Services reference) 'DragFullWindows': HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop, REG_SZ, range 0|1, default 1; 0 = only the outline of the window moves, 1 = the entire window and its contents move; changed by Display > Effects > 'Show window contents while dragging'. Winaero 'Show or hide window contents while dragging': string (REG_SZ) value DragFullWindows, 1 shows contents, 0 shows an outline, sign out and in to apply. Value read as REG_SZ '1' on Windows 11 build 26200.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc787526(v=ws.10)",
        "https://winaero.com/show-hide-window-contents-while-dragging-windows-10/"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Control Panel\\Desktop",
          "name": "DragFullWindows",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "display.adaptive-brightness-off",
      "name": "Turn off adaptive brightness when plugged in",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Stops Windows changing screen brightness by itself on AC power.",
      "description": "Sets the power-plan setting 'Enable adaptive brightness' to 0 on AC power. On PCs with an ambient light sensor, Windows otherwise calculates the brightness from the brightness slider and the surrounding light. With 0 the brightness stays where the slider puts it. Microsoft documents the setting (alias ADAPTBRIGHT, minimum 0, maximum 1) in the Display power subgroup.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Does something only on a PC (normally a laptop or tablet) with an integrated ambient light sensor; on a desktop monitor it has no effect. Only the plugged-in value is changed, battery behaviour is untouched. Microsoft does not document the default (it is set by the PC maker and power plan), so the before-image matters. OEM display utilities and driver features (for example content-adaptive brightness) are separate and can still change brightness. A comfort and colour-consistency preference, not a performance setting.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived) 'ALS-based automatic brightness (adaptive brightness)': name 'Enable adaptive brightness', GUID fbd9aa66-9553-4097-ba44-ed6e9d65eab8, PowerCfg alias ADAPTBRIGHT, minimum 0, maximum 1, 'If enabled, Windows automatically calculates the optimal brightness level based on the default brightness setting and current ambient lighting', Windows Vista and later, Hidden: No. Microsoft Learn 'Display settings overview': Display subgroup GUID 7516b95f-f776-4464-8c53-06167f40cc99, alias SUB_VIDEO.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/hardware/design/dn613878(v=vs.85)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/display-settings"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "7516b95f-f776-4464-8c53-06167f40cc99",
          "setting": "fbd9aa66-9553-4097-ba44-ed6e9d65eab8",
          "name": "Enable adaptive brightness",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "visual.clear-logon-background",
      "name": "Show the sign-in background without blur (policy)",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Draws the sign-in screen background sharp instead of with the acrylic blur.",
      "description": "Enables the policy 'Show clear logon background'. After sign-out or restart, Windows shows the sign-in background image without the acrylic blur it normally applies. The policy text describes only the logon background image; it does not mention the desktop.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "minBuild": 18362,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A cosmetic change; the saving from skipping one blur on the sign-in screen is not documented and is expected to be negligible, so Mesura should not present it as a speed-up. Machine-wide. Microsoft lists the policy for Pro, Enterprise and Education only, so Mesura offers it on those editions (proEdition); whether Windows Home honours the value was not verified. If Transparency effects are already off in Settings, the sign-in blur is already gone and this changes nothing. Windows default: not configured (blur on).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_Logon/DisableAcrylicBackgroundOnLogon: friendly name 'Show clear logon background', Device scope, Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 2004+ and Windows 11 21H2+, key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System, value DisableAcrylicBackgroundOnLogon. Logon.admx/Logon.adml on Windows 11 build 26200: supported on Windows 10 RS6 (1903) and later; 'If you enable this policy, the logon background image shows without blur. If you disable or do not configure this policy, the logon background image adopts the acrylic blur effect.' The ADMX defines no explicit enabled value, so enabling writes the ADMX default, DWORD 1.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-logon"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System",
          "name": "DisableAcrylicBackgroundOnLogon",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "services.gameinput-off",
      "name": "Disable the GameInput service",
      "category": "Optional features",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Disables GameInputSvc, the service behind the GameInput API.",
      "description": "Sets the GameInput Service (GameInputSvc, runs as LocalSystem in its own process) to Disabled. The service lets keyboards, mice, gamepads and other devices work through the GameInput API, Microsoft's newer input API for games. The CIS Windows 11 benchmark (Level 2) recommends it disabled as attack-surface reduction. On the build-26200 PC read for this research it is Manual and stopped.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Games and apps that use the GameInput API lose input through it; legacy APIs (XInput, DirectInput, Raw Input) are separate. Disabling does not prove any game uses GameInput, so test your games. Games, Gaming Services or the GameInput redistributable can reinstall or reconfigure the service, and users report it returning to Automatic after a restart or game update, so Disabled may not stay. The redistributable also installs its own separate service, which this entry does not touch. CIS's rationale (GameInput passes input via DMA, raising the risk of keystroke capture) is the benchmark's claim; it was not verified here, and no performance or latency effect is claimed. The service exists only where GameInput is installed, so on other PCs there is nothing to change. Windows default observed: Manual (read from one PC, not a Microsoft statement). Controller or Xbox-app problems after disabling: undo, or set it back to Manual.",
      "source": "CIS Microsoft Windows 11 benchmark item 5.5 (L2) as published by Tenable: GameInputSvc set to Disabled, runs as LocalSystem, impact 'Input devices will not be able to utilize the GameInput API' (https://www.tenable.com/audits/items/CIS_Microsoft_Windows_11_Stand-alone_v5.0.0_L2.audit:ab1fefae0f11191d7135695aac8a1df1). Microsoft Learn GDK, GameInput introduction, describes the API and its role (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/features/common/input/overviews/input-overview). borncity.com describes the service and suggests disabling it for log noise. Default Manual read locally (Get-Service, build 26200).",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.tenable.com/audits/items/CIS_Microsoft_Windows_11_Stand-alone_v5.0.0_L2.audit:ab1fefae0f11191d7135695aac8a1df1",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/gdk/docs/features/common/input/overviews/input-overview",
        "https://borncity.com/win/2025/10/01/windows-11-event-viewer-flooded-with-gameinput-service-reconfigured-messages-sept-2025/"
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "name": "GameInputSvc",
          "start": "disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "input.pointer-speed-middle",
      "name": "Set pointer speed to the middle notch",
      "category": "Input",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Resets pointer speed to Windows' default, so motion isn't scaled up or down.",
      "description": "Sets the Windows pointer-speed slider (value 1 to 20) to 10, its default and the middle notch (6 of 11 in Settings). Microsoft documents that at 10 the speed step adds no further scaling to mouse motion. With 'Enhance pointer precision' off (a separate Mesura setting) the pointer then moves in direct proportion to the mouse's counts, which is what people usually mean by '1:1'. This is not a pixel-perfect or raw-input mode.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Overwrites a speed you chose on purpose; if your DPI is low you may want a higher value, and the pointer will then feel slower on the desktop. Only affects the Windows pointer (desktop, menus, games that read the cursor position); games and apps that use Raw Input or DirectInput ignore this slider and 'Enhance pointer precision' entirely. With 'Enhance pointer precision' ON the result is NOT 1:1: the acceleration curve still changes the gain with movement speed. Doesn't change latency or frame rate. Takes effect after sign-out, or after moving the slider once.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn SystemParametersInfo: SPI_SETMOUSESPEED is an integer 1 (slowest) to 20 (fastest), 10 is the default. Microsoft Learn mouse_event Remarks: 'The default value is 10, which results in no additional modification to the mouse motion.' Registry name and type (REG_SZ MouseSensitivity under HKCU\\Control Panel\\Mouse, 1-20, default 10) come from several tutorial sources found by search (ElevenForum, Windows Central, Appuals-type guides) and were read on the author's own PC as REG_SZ '10'; Microsoft does not document the value name, hence 'community'. Mechanism of 'Enhance pointer precision' off and raw-input bypass: How-To Geek and a reverse-engineering comment thread on donewmouseaccel.blogspot.com (secondary).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-systemparametersinfoa",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-mouse_event",
        "https://www.howtogeek.com/321763/what-is-enhance-pointer-precision-in-windows/",
        "https://donewmouseaccel.blogspot.com/2009/06/out-of-sync-and-upside-down-windows.html"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Control Panel\\Mouse",
          "name": "MouseSensitivity",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "10",
          "absentMatches": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "input.touchpad-most-sensitive",
      "name": "Set touchpad sensitivity to 'Most sensitive'",
      "category": "Input",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Touchpad no longer ignores touches for a moment after you press a key.",
      "description": "Precision Touchpads briefly suppress taps and movement after keyboard activity (Accidental Activation Prevention). 'Most sensitive' is the setting where those suppression timeouts are zero, so the touchpad reacts at once after typing or WASD. Windows stores it as AAPThreshold, 0 being the most sensitive (default 2, medium).",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Laptops with a Windows Precision Touchpad only; does nothing on desktops or on older, non-Precision touchpads. Palm rejection while typing is weaker, so accidental cursor jumps and taps are more likely. A device-wide policy value AAPDisabled in HKLM, if set by the PC maker, overrides the Settings slider. Not a performance setting.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Precision touchpad tuning': under HKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PrecisionTouchPad, 'Touchpad sensitivity' = AAPThreshold, DWORD, range 0-4, default 2, 'Lower values correspond to higher sensitivity (meaning 0 indicates highest sensitivity)'; the same page states 'Maximum sensitivity means AAP timeouts are zero and AAP is not in effect'. Whether sign-out is needed is not stated by Microsoft; community guides advise a restart.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/design/component-guidelines/touchpad-tuning-guidelines"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PrecisionTouchPad",
          "name": "AAPThreshold",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ],
      "minBuild": 10586
    },
    {
      "id": "input.touchpad-off-with-mouse",
      "name": "Turn the touchpad off while a mouse is connected",
      "category": "Input",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Ignores touchpad input whenever an external mouse is plugged in.",
      "description": "Clears 'Leave touchpad on when a mouse is connected'. Windows then ignores all touchpad input while an external mouse is present, so a palm brushing the pad during a game can't move the cursor or click. The touchpad works again when the mouse is unplugged.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Laptops with a Precision Touchpad only. Some keyboard and mouse combinations (wireless receivers that present a mouse and a keyboard together) may be treated as a mouse and switch the touchpad off even if you only use the keyboard; Microsoft lists an IgnoredExternalMice exception list for that case. You lose touchpad gestures while the mouse is attached. Not a performance setting.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Precision touchpad tuning': 'Leave touchpad on when a mouse is connected' = LeaveOnWithMouse, DWORD 0-1, default 1, under HKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PrecisionTouchPad; 'When inactive, any input produced by the touchpad is ignored'. The same page describes the IgnoredExternalMice exemption list for keyboard/mouse collections detected as a mouse.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/design/component-guidelines/touchpad-tuning-guidelines"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PrecisionTouchPad",
          "name": "LeaveOnWithMouse",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ],
      "minBuild": 10586
    },
    {
      "id": "input.touchpad-follow-mouse-accel",
      "name": "Make the touchpad follow the 'Enhance pointer precision' setting",
      "category": "Input",
      "impact": "interface",
      "summary": "Touchpad pointer stops accelerating when mouse acceleration is off.",
      "description": "By default the Precision Touchpad always applies pointer acceleration, whatever the 'Enhance pointer precision' checkbox says. This sets HonorMouseAccelSetting to 1 so the touchpad follows the checkbox like a mouse does. Together with Mesura's 'Turn off Enhance pointer precision' the touchpad pointer then moves in proportion to finger travel.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Laptops with a Precision Touchpad only. A touchpad has a small surface and relies on acceleration to cross the screen; with acceleration off, large moves need several swipes or a higher touchpad Cursor speed. Has no effect if 'Enhance pointer precision' is still on; in that case the touchpad uses the mouse acceleration curve, so its feel may change. Documented by Microsoft as a setting with no user interface and read only by Windows 10 1511 and later; no second source or test confirms it still takes effect on current Windows 11 builds. Not a performance setting.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Precision touchpad tuning', 'Settings with no user interface': 'Mouse acceleration' = HonorMouseAccelSetting, DWORD 0-1, default 0, Windows 10 1511+; it 'specifies whether the mouse motion produced by the touchpad honors the user's mouse acceleration setting as returned by SystemParametersInfo(SPI_GETMOUSE). If 0, the mouse motion will always have acceleration applied.' Path is under HKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PrecisionTouchPad (same page).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/design/component-guidelines/touchpad-tuning-guidelines"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PrecisionTouchPad",
          "name": "HonorMouseAccelSetting",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ],
      "minBuild": 10586
    },
    {
      "id": "network.interrupt-moderation-off",
      "name": "Turn off network card interrupt moderation",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Network card raises an interrupt per packet instead of batching them.",
      "description": "Interrupt moderation makes a network card wait for more packets, or for a timer, before it interrupts the CPU. Microsoft lists switching it off as a tuning step for the lowest possible latency and says it costs CPU time. This sets the standard NDIS keyword *InterruptModeration to 0 on every adapter whose driver exposes that standard keyword in its Advanced properties; Windows' documented default is 1 (enabled).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Costs CPU: a community test (one gigabit Intel adapter, Windows 10 1909, 30 s of about 900 Mbps TCP) counted about 92,000 interrupts at the most aggressive moderation setting and about 2,650,000 with moderation off. The source does not say whether these are per second or totals, so read it only as a ratio of roughly 29 times more interrupts. The gain is on a microsecond scale; no ping or FPS gain is measured or promised. Only adapters whose driver exposes the standard *InterruptModeration property are touched; many drivers use their own vendor keyword and are left alone. Wi-Fi adapters mostly do not expose it (none of PC-000's does, so this was not tested on real hardware). The adapter reads the value when it restarts, so a restart is needed. Do not use on a laptop on battery or a weak CPU.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Enumeration Keywords': *InterruptModeration 0 = Disabled, 1 = Enabled (Default); 'Interrupt Moderation' (drivers turn it on by default unless the keyword disables it); 'Network Adapter Performance Tuning': 'Disable the Interrupt Moderation setting for network card drivers that require the lowest possible latency ... this configuration can use more CPU time and it represents a tradeoff'. Net class GUID 4d36e972-... from 'System-Defined Device Setup Classes'. Keyword values are REG_SZ (read on PC-000: *WakeOnMagicPacket is a String). Interrupt counts from the community djdallmann GamingPCSetup network research page (single adapter, not Microsoft data).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/enumeration-keywords",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/interrupt-moderation",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/network-subsystem/net-sub-performance-tuning-nics",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/install/system-defined-device-setup-classes-available-to-vendors",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-network-devices",
        "https://djdallmann.github.io/GamingPCSetup/CONTENT/RESEARCH/NETWORK"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*InterruptModeration",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\params\\*InterruptModeration"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.ethernet-eee-off",
      "name": "Turn off Energy-Efficient Ethernet",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Stops a wired link entering low-power idle between packets.",
      "description": "Energy-Efficient Ethernet (IEEE 802.3az) lets a wired link drop into a low-power idle state when there is no traffic and wake when a packet arrives. Microsoft defines the standard keyword *EEE (0 = Disabled, 1 = Enabled, default 1). This sets it to 0 on adapters whose driver exposes it, so the link stays fully powered.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Windows documents the keyword and its default, not a latency or FPS benefit; the wake delay of low-power idle is described only in secondary sources (microseconds), so the benefit is unverified. Uses slightly more power on an idle link. Does nothing on Wi-Fi and on wired drivers that use a vendor-specific EEE keyword instead of the standard *EEE (they are left alone). Not tested on real hardware: PC-000 has only a Wi-Fi adapter. Needs an adapter restart, so a reboot. Community advice (not Microsoft) often lists turning EEE off when a link drops or flaps with a particular switch; Mesura does not claim it fixes that.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Standardized INF Keywords for Power Management': '*EEE: a value that describes whether the device should enable IEEE 802.3az energy-efficient ethernet'; table: 0 Disabled, 1 (Default) Enabled. Net class GUID from 'System-Defined Device Setup Classes'. Keyword values are REG_SZ (read on PC-000).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-power-management",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/install/system-defined-device-setup-classes-available-to-vendors",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/enumeration-keywords"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*EEE",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\params\\*EEE"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.tcp-ack-every-segment",
      "name": "Acknowledge every TCP segment immediately",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Acknowledge every received TCP segment at once instead of every second one.",
      "description": "By default Windows TCP acknowledges every second segment it receives, or sooner if a timer expires (TcpAckFrequency default 2). Setting TcpAckFrequency to 1 on a network interface makes Windows acknowledge every received segment immediately. Microsoft documents the value and says it does not recommend changing the default without careful study.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Microsoft does not recommend it. More acknowledgements mean more small packets and more CPU work during large downloads. It changes how this PC acknowledges data it receives, not Nagle's algorithm on data it sends (no documented Microsoft switch for that); games mostly use UDP, which it does not affect. KB 328890 describes the older 200 ms delayed-ACK timer; on Windows 11 build 26200 'netsh int tcp show supplemental' reports a 40 ms timeout and frequency 2 (read on PC-000), so any gain is small. The Learn page names no Windows version, and whether Windows 10 or 11 still honours this value was not verified. Written only to adapter keys under Interfaces that exist when applied, including disconnected ones; an adapter added later or reinstalled gets a new key without it. No FPS or ping gain is measured.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'New registry entry for controlling TCP ACK' (KB 328890): HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\\Interfaces\\<Interface GUID>, TcpAckFrequency, REG_DWORD, valid 0-255, default 2; '1' acknowledges every packet; 0 is invalid and treated as 2; 'Microsoft does not recommend changing the default value without careful study'. Delayed-ACK values on this build read locally with netsh.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/registry-entry-control-tcp-acknowledgment-behavior"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\\Interfaces\\*",
          "name": "TcpAckFrequency",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.nic-flow-control-off",
      "name": "Turn off network adapter flow control",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Adapter stops sending and honouring Ethernet pause frames",
      "description": "Ethernet flow control lets the adapter send pause frames to ask the switch to stop sending, and obey pause frames from the switch. The standardized keyword *FlowControl = 0 means 'Tx & Rx Disabled'. Microsoft's keyword table lists 3 (Rx & Tx) as the client default and 0 as the server default for driver INFs, and notes that a NIC pushing pause frames (its example is a machine attached to a kernel debugger) can make most switches temporarily take the network down for other computers on it; Windows therefore disables flow control itself while kernel debugging is on. This writes the keyword on every adapter whose driver declares it (Ndi\\Params\\*FlowControl exists).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "No source here measures a latency or FPS gain; the documented effect is only that pause frames stop. With flow control off the PC cannot ask the switch to pause during a burst and ignores pauses from the switch; on a congested link that can mean more dropped packets and TCP recovery (reasoning, not a measurement), and lossless uses such as storage networks on managed switches may depend on pause frames. On a typical home network the difference is rarely visible. The 3 / 0 defaults are what Microsoft tells driver INFs to use for client / server; some drivers define their own default or an 'Auto Negotiation' value (4), so the real previous value is whatever is recorded. Some drivers expose a vendor-specific enumeration instead and ignore this keyword. No adapter on the reference PC declares this keyword, so it has not been exercised here. Adapter restart or reboot needed; link drops briefly.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Enumeration Keywords: *FlowControl 0 (Server Default) = Tx & Rx Disabled, 1 = Tx, 2 = Rx, 3 (Client Default) = Rx & Tx, 4 = Auto Negotiation; the note that pause frames from an adapter can make most switches take down the network for the other computers. Set-NetAdapterAdvancedProperty documentation uses 'Flow Control' / '*Flo*rol' as an example and states many advanced properties need an adapter restart.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/enumeration-keywords",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/netadapter/set-netadapteradvancedproperty"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*FlowControl",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\Params\\*FlowControl"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.nic-selective-suspend-off",
      "name": "Turn off network adapter selective suspend",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Stops Windows idling the adapter between traffic bursts (NDIS drivers)",
      "description": "NDIS selective suspend lets Windows put an idle network adapter into a low-power state after an idle time-out (5 seconds by default, 3 seconds with the screen off) and wake it when traffic arrives. The standardized keyword *SelectiveSuspend = 0 tells the miniport driver not to advertise selective suspend at all. This writes the keyword on every adapter whose driver declares it (Ndi\\Params\\*SelectiveSuspend exists).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft marks the keyword as for traditional NDIS miniport drivers only and deprecated for NetAdapterCx drivers, so adapters on NetAdapterCx drivers either lack it or ignore it. Which adapters declare it depends on the driver; none of the adapters on the reference PC (an Intel Wi-Fi 6 AX201 and virtual adapters) do, so there the entry changes nothing. Cost: higher idle power, so a laptop on battery loses runtime (hence the noBattery requirement). The benefit (no wake-up delay after idle) is plausible but not measured here. Default is enabled (1). Adapter restart or reboot needed. This is separate from the USB hub setting 'USB selective suspend' in the power plan.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Standardized INF Keywords for NDIS Selective Suspend: *SelectiveSuspend 0 = Disabled, 1 (Default) = Enabled; if 0 the miniport must not advertise any selective suspend capability; *SSIdleTimeout default 5 s, *SSIdleTimeoutScreenOff default 3 s; keywords are for NDIS miniport drivers only and deprecated in NetAdapterCx.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-ndis-selective-suspend"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*SelectiveSuspend",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\Params\\*SelectiveSuspend"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.nic-wake-on-pattern-off",
      "name": "Wake from sleep only on a magic packet",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "power",
      "summary": "Stops packet patterns from waking the PC from sleep; the magic packet still works.",
      "description": "By default the adapter may wake the PC when a packet matches a pattern the OS programmed (the keyword *WakeOnPattern, default 1) or when it receives a magic packet (*WakeOnMagicPacket, default 1). This sets *WakeOnPattern = 0 on every adapter whose driver declares it (Ndi\\Params\\*WakeOnPattern exists), which is the keyword behind 'Wake on pattern match' in the adapter's Advanced tab. The magic-packet value is left unchanged.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noModernStandby"
      ],
      "limitations": "Loses wake-on-pattern: wakes by ordinary traffic that matches an OS pattern (such as some remote-access or file-sharing wake cases) no longer work. Wake-on-LAN by magic packet is not touched, but it keeps working only if *WakeOnMagicPacket, the adapter's 'allow this device to wake the computer' setting and the BIOS/UEFI WoL setting allow it. On Modern Standby PCs pattern-match wake is required: Microsoft calls it essential to keeping the network connected during modern standby, and Intel states that with Wake on Pattern Match disabled the system does not enter Modern Standby; hence the entry requires noModernStandby and is skipped on such PCs (the reference PC is one). The benefit (fewer unwanted wake-ups) is a preference, not a measured performance effect. Default is enabled (1). Adapter restart or reboot needed.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Standardized INF Keywords for Power Management: *WakeOnPattern 0 = Disabled, 1 (Default) = Enabled, 'wake the computer when a network packet matches a specified pattern'; *WakeOnMagicPacket 0/1, default 1. Read-only check on a Windows 11 PC: the Intel AX201 Wi-Fi driver declares *WakeOnPattern and *WakeOnMagicPacket under Ndi\\Params and stores the value as REG_SZ '1'.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-power-management"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*WakeOnPattern",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\Params\\*WakeOnPattern"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.teredo-off",
      "name": "Turn off Teredo tunnelling",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Removes the Teredo IPv6-over-UDP tunnel; breaks Xbox network features on PC.",
      "description": "Teredo wraps IPv6 in IPv4 UDP so a PC behind NAT can reach other IPv6 peers. This sets the 'Set Teredo State' policy to Disabled, which Microsoft documents as 'No Teredo interfaces are present on the host'. Microsoft's IPv6 article lists this policy as the way to turn off the IPv6 transition tunnels.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft's Xbox support answer says that without a Teredo IP address you cannot use Xbox Live party chat or multiplayer on the PC; some PC games that use Xbox Live networking can lose chat or matchmaking. Do not use it if you play Xbox Live or Game Pass PC multiplayer. Microsoft's IPv6 article says ISATAP and Teredo are disabled by default in Windows, and on this machine Teredo already reports type 'disabled' (netsh interface teredo show state), so on many PCs the setting changes nothing. No speed gain is claimed. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_tcpip, Teredo_State: values Default (default state is Client) / Disabled ('No Teredo interfaces are present on the host') / Client / Enterprise Client; value name Teredo_State (string) confirmed in shipped tcpip.admx. Microsoft Q&A (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/a/4553681): without a Teredo IP address you cannot use Xbox Live party chat or multiplayer; 'netsh interface Teredo set state disable' / 'type=default'. Microsoft KB 929852 lists Teredo State = Disabled as the policy way to remove tunnel interfaces.",
      "sources": [
