      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Stops the Windows DNS client resolving .local names over multicast DNS.",
      "description": "Since Windows 10 1703 the DNS client can resolve names over multicast DNS (mDNS, UDP 5353). This sets the DNS Client policy 'Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol' to 0 (disabled), the value the CIS Windows 11 benchmark recommends, so the Windows resolver is meant to stop using mDNS. Microsoft's own help text only says a disabled policy means 'locally configured settings', so Microsoft does not state the effect itself. Microsoft's networking team says turning off the DNS client's mDNS only affects that resolver: other software (browsers, Teams, printer drivers) can run their own, and the firewall rule 'mDNS (UDP-In)' is Microsoft's recommended control.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 26100,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft warns that turning mDNS off can break wireless screen sharing (Miracast) and printer discovery. Microsoft is moving Windows away from NetBIOS and LLMNR toward mDNS, so this switches off the multicast name protocol Windows is keeping. Names like printer.local or nas.local can stop resolving through Windows. It does not block mDNS traffic from other apps. The value name comes from Microsoft's shipped ADMX, the CIS benchmark and the DNS service's own registry reader, not from a Microsoft Learn reference page; only Windows 11 24H2/25H2 (build 26200) was checked, so it is offered from build 26100. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart.",
      "source": "Shipped dnsclient.admx / dnsclient.adml on Windows 11 26200: policy DNS_MDNS, key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient, value EnableMDNS, enabled=1, disabled=0; text 'If you enable this policy, the DNS client will use mDNS protocol. If you disable ... or do not configure ... the DNS client will use locally configured settings.' Microsoft Tech Community 'mDNS in the Enterprise' (read via the argonsys.com reproduction): native mDNS since Windows 10 1703; the registry setting disables only the DNSCache resolver; recommended control is the Defender Firewall 'mDNS (UDP-In)' rules; disabling can break Miracast and printer access. CIS Windows 11 benchmark item 'Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol' = Disabled (search result only).",
      "sources": [
        "https://argonsys.com/microsoft-cloud/library/mdns-in-the-enterprise/",
