      "id": "network.smart-multihomed-dns-off",
      "name": "Turn off smart multi-homed name resolution",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Windows asks DNS on all networks first, then LLMNR, then NetBIOS, one step at a time.",
      "description": "By default a PC with more than one active network (Ethernet plus Wi-Fi, a VPN, a virtual switch) sends DNS, LLMNR and NetBIOS lookups to all of them in parallel and accepts the first positive answer, using binding order if several answer. This sets the DNS Client policy 'Turn off smart multi-homed name resolution': DNS queries go to all networks first, LLMNR only if DNS fails, NetBT only if LLMNR fails. It is the documented way to make the order predictable on a multi-network PC.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft describes this setting as an optimisation switch, not a privacy one. Whether it limits where a name query is sent (for example with a VPN) is plausible but not stated by the sources I read. Lookups that need the fallback protocols can be slower, because they wait for the earlier step to fail. On a PC with a single network connection nothing changes. No speed gain is claimed. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_DnsClient, DNS_SmartMultiHomedNameResolution: 'Turn off smart multi-homed name resolution', value DisableSmartNameResolution; enabling = no optimisations, DNS across all networks first, then LLMNR, then NetBT; disabling or not configured = optimised parallel queries. Value name and enabledValue 1 confirmed in the shipped dnsclient.admx on Windows 11 26200.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-dnsclient"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient",
          "name": "DisableSmartNameResolution",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
