          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.ask-before-installing",
      "name": "Ask before installing Windows updates",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Updates download in the background but wait for you to install them.",
      "description": "Turns on the 'Configure Automatic Updates' policy with option 3, 'Auto download and notify for install': Windows finds and downloads updates in the background, then notifies you that they are ready; you install them from Settings > Windows Update. Updates the policy covers are not installed, and so do not restart the PC, until you choose.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 17763,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "You become responsible for installing security fixes: if you ignore the prompt the PC stays unpatched, which is why Microsoft recommends leaving updates automatic (the default). A Group Policy value; Microsoft lists Configure Automatic Updates for Pro, Enterprise and Education, so on Home it does not apply. Settings shows 'Some settings are managed by your organization'. Microsoft's help text calls option 3 the 'Default setting', while the Policy CSP gives auto-install-and-restart as the default; this entry takes the documented meaning of the value, not the wording of the default. Compliance deadlines, if configured elsewhere, can still force an install. Reports from 2025 say Microsoft's automatic rollout of a new feature release to unmanaged PCs still downloads and prompts regardless of such settings; this value was not tested against that, and the pin-to-version entry is the documented way to hold a feature release. Whether current Windows 11 builds honour option 3 exactly as the Windows 10-era text describes was not tested here; confirm on a test PC. NoAutoUpdate is set to 0 (Automatic Updates on) so a leftover 1 from a tweak tool cannot switch updating off. Not for kiosks or PCs nobody watches.",
      "source": "Learn waas-wu-settings 'Configuring Automatic Updates by editing the registry': HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU NoAutoUpdate 0 = enabled (default), 1 = disabled; AUOptions 3 = 'Automatically download and notify of installation'; Learn waas-wufb-group-policy: 'We recommend that you allow to update automatically--this is the default behavior'; Windows 11 ADMX (AutoUpdateCfg: valueName NoAutoUpdate enabled=0, enum AUOptions 2/3/4/5/7); Learn Policy CSP Update AllowAutoUpdate (default 2 = auto install and restart; Pro/Enterprise/Education; warns it should be used only where compliance requires).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-wu-settings",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-wufb-group-policy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-update"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU",
          "name": "NoAutoUpdate",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0",
          "absentMatches": true
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU",
          "name": "AUOptions",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.defer-feature-updates-365",
      "name": "Delay Windows feature updates by 365 days",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "New Windows feature releases reach this PC a year after release; monthly fixes continue.",
      "description": "Turns on 'Select when Preview Builds and Feature Updates are received' with a deferral of 365 days, the maximum for the General Availability Channel. A feature update is offered only once it has been out for that many days (offer date = release date + deferral). Quality (monthly) updates are a separate policy and are not delayed by it.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 17763,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A Group Policy value; Microsoft lists it for Pro, Enterprise and Education, so on Home it does not apply. Microsoft's own pages disagree on Windows 11: the Policy CSP lists DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays for Windows 10 1607 and later with no Windows 11 limit and the Windows 11 ADMX ships it outside the Legacy category, but the 'Configure Windows Update client policies' page marks this GP row 'legacy, not applicable for Windows 11'. Treat it as unproven on Windows 11 and check on a test PC; the pin-to-version entry is the documented route on Windows 11. Ignored while a target feature version is set. New features and hardware support delivered through feature updates arrive up to a year late. Per Microsoft's supported-versions table each Home/Pro release is serviced about 24 months, so a 365-day delay stays inside support. No performance gain is claimed.",
      "source": "Learn waas-configure-wufb: GP table 'DeferFeatureUpdates = 1: defer', 'DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodinDays 0-365', 'up to 365 days from their release', offer date = release date + deferral; Learn Policy CSP Update DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays (0-365, default 0, Windows 10 1607 and later, Pro/Enterprise/Education); Windows 11 ADMX build 26200 (DeferFeatureUpdates policy, element DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays 0-365, help text 'up to 365 days for the General Availability Channel'); Learn waas-wufb-group-policy (deferrals not in effect when a target version is set). Learn marks the GP row legacy for Windows 11: conflict noted in limitations.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-configure-wufb",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-update",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-wufb-group-policy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/release-health/supported-versions-windows-client"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "DeferFeatureUpdates",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate",
          "name": "DeferFeatureUpdatesPeriodInDays",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "365"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.no-store-auto-update",
      "name": "Turn off automatic Microsoft Store app updates",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Microsoft Store apps stop updating on their own until you update them.",
      "description": "Turns on the Store policy 'Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates': apps from the Microsoft Store are no longer downloaded and installed in the background. You update them yourself from the Store's Downloads page. While the policy is set it decides in place of the setting the user can change in the Store.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Store apps (and any app updated through the Store) stay unpatched until you update them, including security fixes, so this is a trade-off, not a tuning gain; no performance gain is claimed. Microsoft lists the policy (Policy CSP ApplicationManagement/AllowAppStoreAutoUpdate) for Pro, Enterprise and Education, Windows 10 1507 and later, with no Windows 11 limit, so on Home it does not apply. When the policy is not set the Store's own user setting decides (Microsoft's help text). Drivers and Windows updates are not affected. Microsoft's CSP page calls 0 'most restricted'; the registry value for 'off' is 2.",
      "source": "Learn Policy CSP ApplicationManagement, AllowAppStoreAutoUpdate: GP 'Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore, value AutoDownload, 0 = not allowed, default 'not configured', Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 1507 and later; Windows 11 build 26200 WindowsStore.admx (DisableAutoInstall: enabled = 2, disabled = 4; help text 'If you enable this setting, the automatic download and installation of app updates is turned off... If you don't configure this setting... determined by a registry setting that the user can change using Settings in the Microsoft Store'). Community guides (MajorGeeks, Hexnode) give the same value 2.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-applicationmanagement",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/configuration/store/"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore",
          "name": "AutoDownload",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "updates.limit-background-download-bandwidth",
      "name": "Cap background update downloads at 25% of bandwidth",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "network",
      "summary": "Delivery Optimization uses at most a quarter of your bandwidth for background downloads.",
      "description": "Sets Delivery Optimization's 'Maximum Background Download Bandwidth (percentage)' to 25. Delivery Optimization downloads Windows updates and Store content in the background; this caps it at a quarter of the available download bandwidth across all such downloads. By default (0) Delivery Optimization adjusts to the available bandwidth on its own.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 17134,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Background update and Store downloads take longer. Microsoft documents the limit and its meaning but not any effect on games or latency: whether it helps depends on your connection and no gain is claimed. The 25 is Mesura's choice, not a Microsoft recommendation; the policy accepts 0 to 100. The limit applies to downloads from Microsoft's servers and Connected Cache and from non-local peers, not to downloads from PCs on your own network, and Microsoft suggests the absolute KB/s limits for slow connections. A Group Policy value; Microsoft lists it for Pro, Enterprise and Education (Windows 10 1803 and later), so on Home it does not apply.",
      "source": "Learn Policy CSP DeliveryOptimization, DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth: maximum background download bandwidth as a percentage of available download bandwidth, range 0-100, default 0, key SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeliveryOptimization, Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 1803 and later; Learn Delivery Optimization reference: 'default value of 0 means that Delivery Optimization dynamically adjusts', limit applies to Microsoft CDNs, Connected Cache and non-local peers, not LAN peers, and absolute KB/s limits are recommended for low bandwidth. Windows 11 DeliveryOptimization.admx confirms the value name.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-deliveryoptimization",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/do/waas-delivery-optimization-reference"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeliveryOptimization",
          "name": "DOPercentageMaxBackgroundBandwidth",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "25"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.automatic-maintenance-off",
      "name": "Turn off Automatic Maintenance",
      "category": "Windows Update",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Windows stops running its scheduled idle-time maintenance.",
      "description": "Sets the MaintenanceDisabled value that tells the Task Scheduler's automatic-maintenance machinery not to run. Automatic Maintenance is the Windows facility that runs opportunistic background tasks (Microsoft: tasks that keep the system healthy) when the PC is idle and on AC power. On Windows 11 build 26200 about 45 scheduled tasks are registered for it, among them drive optimisation and TRIM, registry backup, the scheduled System Restore task, Storage Sense, component cleanup and search-index maintenance. With it off, those scheduled runs do not happen.",
      "risk": "undocumented",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Microsoft documents what Automatic Maintenance is but not this value; the value, its effect and its default (absent = maintenance on) come from two independent community guides (Winaero, Geekrewind), which say a restart is needed and that manual start of maintenance is also removed. Static check on Windows 11 build 26200: the Task Scheduler engine (ubpm.dll) contains the name MaintenanceDisabled beside the Schedule\\Maintenance key; the effect itself was not run. Tasks that stop include drive optimisation (defrag and SSD TRIM), registry backup, the automatic System Restore task, Storage Sense, component cleanup, disk-check scan and search-index maintenance; they must be run by hand. The Defender scan task and the Windows Update orchestrator tasks are not among the maintenance tasks on that build, so they are not expected to stop, but this was not tested. The guides treat disabling it as a troubleshooting step ('most users should not disable it'). No performance gain was measured. Another tool's equivalent was excluded in the 2026-10-25 audit for lack of a Microsoft statement; this entry is offered only as opt-in, with that gap stated. The value is a normal REG_DWORD.",
      "source": "Community, two independent guides agree: Winaero 'How to disable Automatic Maintenance in Windows 10' (fetched: HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Schedule\\Maintenance, MaintenanceDisabled REG_DWORD 1 disables, 0 or delete re-enables, restart needed, 'Most users should not disable Automatic Maintenance') and Geekrewind guide for Windows 11 (fetched: same key/value/data, restart, lists what maintenance covers, removes manual start). Microsoft Learn 'Automatic maintenance (Task Scheduler)' (fetched) documents the mechanism: opportunistic tasks run when the PC is idle and on AC power. A WinHelpOnline/ElevenForum search result gives the same value but the pages returned 403 and are not relied on. Local check 2026-10-29: the value is absent on PC-000.",
      "sources": [
        "https://winaero.com/how-to-disable-automatic-maintenance-in-windows-10",
        "https://geekrewind.com/guide-for-enabling-or-disabling-automatic-maintenance-in-windows-11/",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/taskschd/task-maintenence"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Schedule\\Maintenance",
          "name": "MaintenanceDisabled",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "services.compat-assistant-off",
      "name": "Turn off the Program Compatibility Assistant",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Stops the Program Compatibility Assistant service (PcaSvc) from starting.",
      "description": "The Program Compatibility Assistant service watches programs you install and run and detects known compatibility problems; Windows then shows its 'this program might not have installed correctly' style prompts and offers compatibility settings. This entry sets the service to Disabled. Microsoft's Windows IoT Enterprise and Windows Server 2016 service guides list it as 'OK to disable' for systems that do not use it.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Windows default is Automatic (Delayed Start) with trigger starts. Microsoft documents that with the service stopped the Program Compatibility Assistant does not function: Windows no longer detects known compatibility problems or offers its fix prompts, which matters for older games and installers. Whether compatibility settings you already applied keep working was not checked. No measured performance gain: the service is lightweight and the benefit is unverified. Keep it if you run old games or installers regularly. Nothing else was found that depends on it.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Guidance on configuring system services - Windows IoT Enterprise' (updated 2026-06): PcaSvc 'OK to disable', 'PCA monitors programs installed and run by the user and detects known compatibility problems. If this service is stopped, PCA doesn't function properly'; the Server 2016 guide gives the same 'OK to disable'. Default Automatic (Delayed Start, Trigger Start) per smartpcutilities.com Windows 11 Pro list and the service key on a build 26200 PC. https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-services/security-guidelines-for-disabling-system-services-in-windows-server https://www.smartpcutilities.com/windows-11-default-services-configuration.html",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-services/security-guidelines-for-disabling-system-services-in-windows-server",
        "https://www.smartpcutilities.com/windows-11-default-services-configuration.html"
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "name": "PcaSvc",
          "start": "disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "services.connected-devices-off",
      "name": "Turn off the Connected Devices Platform services",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Stops the Connected Devices Platform services (CDPSvc, CDPUserSvc) from starting.",
      "description": "The Connected Devices Platform service and its per-user companion let Windows connect, manage and control nearby and linked devices (Microsoft names mobile, Xbox, HoloLens and smart/IoT devices; 'Share things with nearby devices' is its documented example). This entry sets both to Disabled. Microsoft's Windows IoT Enterprise guide lists both as 'OK to disable'; its Windows Server 2016 guide says the same for CDPUserSvc but gives no guidance for CDPSvc. It complements, and does not replace, the existing cross-device policy entry.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Windows default is Automatic for both (CDPSvc delayed, with trigger starts). Nearby sharing and other features that connect this PC to phones, consoles and other devices stop working. Which other features (Phone Link, Cast, Dynamic Lock, Night Light) depend on it was not confirmed by a Microsoft source; Microsoft warns that apps depending on a disabled per-user service might not work correctly. CDPUserSvc is a per-user service template: Windows still creates it at sign-in, in a stopped and disabled state, and a copy that is already running stops at the next sign-out or restart. Undoing the change takes full effect at the next sign-in or restart. Benefit is unverified (less background activity, not measured). Overlaps with privacy.no-cross-device-experiences, which removes the same feature at policy level.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Guidance on configuring system services - Windows IoT Enterprise' (updated 2026-06): CDPSvc and CDPUserSvc both 'OK to disable'; Microsoft Learn 'Per-user services': CDPUserSvc default Automatic, dependencies Network Connection Broker, RPC, TCP/IP; templates are disabled by setting the Start value to 4 and Windows then creates the instance stopped and disabled. CDPSvc default Automatic (Delayed Start, Trigger Start) per smartpcutilities.com. https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/application-management/per-user-services-in-windows https://www.smartpcutilities.com/windows-11-default-services-configuration.html",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/iot/iot-enterprise/optimize/services",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/application-management/per-user-services-in-windows",
        "https://www.smartpcutilities.com/windows-11-default-services-configuration.html"
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "name": "CDPSvc",
          "start": "disabled"
        },
        {
          "name": "CDPUserSvc",
          "start": "disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.auto-end-tasks",
      "name": "End unresponsive apps automatically at shutdown",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "At sign-out or shutdown, apps that don't respond are ended instead of asking you.",
      "description": "AutoEndTasks controls what Windows does with user programs that do not close when you sign out or shut down. With 0 (the default) Windows waits and then shows a dialog saying the app did not respond. With 1 it ends such processes automatically once the wait expires (WaitToKillAppTimeout).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "Unsaved work can be lost: a program that is waiting for you to answer a 'Save changes?' prompt, or is still writing a file, can be ended without a chance to finish. It speeds up shutdown only when something is blocking it; it does nothing for a normal clean shutdown. Microsoft's description is from the Windows 2000 reference; the modern 'This app is preventing shutdown' screen is not described there, so the exact user-visible behaviour on Windows 10/11 is unverified. Do not combine with a very short WaitToKillAppTimeout if you work with unsaved documents. Type is a string. A missing value means 0. Applies at the next sign-out.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived Windows 2000 resource kit, 'AutoEndTasks'): HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop, REG_SZ, range 0|1, default 0; 'Determines whether user processes end automatically when the user either logs off or shuts down'; 0 = the system waits and, past HungAppTimeout, shows the End Task dialog; 1 = processes end automatically. 'WaitToKillAppTimeout' page: if AutoEndTasks is 1 the system ends the process automatically when the timeout expires.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-2000-server/cc978604(v=technet.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-2000-server/cc978624(v=technet.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-2000-server/cc978614(v=technet.10)"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Control Panel\\Desktop",
          "name": "AutoEndTasks",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "startup.no-startup-app-delay",
      "name": "Remove the delay before startup apps launch",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "system",
      "summary": "Skips Explorer's built-in wait before it starts Startup-folder apps.",
      "description": "After sign-in Explorer holds back the programs in your Startup folder for a while so the desktop can finish loading. Setting StartupDelayInMSec to 0 under Explorer\\Serialize is reported by Windows 10 guides to shorten that wait. Microsoft does not document the value, and its effect on Windows 11 is not confirmed.",
      "risk": "undocumented",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "limitations": "It only moves when startup apps launch; it does not reduce how many there are or how long they take, and launching many at once can make the desktop sluggish just after sign-in (Neowin warns boot can feel longer with many startup items). The delay cannot be removed completely (Winaero). The sources are Windows 10 guides; a Microsoft Q&A thread of Windows 11 23H2 users reports that a separate WaitForIdleState=0 value was also needed there, which is user-reported and not in Microsoft documentation, so this entry's effect on Windows 11 is unverified. A Windows update may delete the Serialize key again. The Serialize key may not exist; Mesura creates it. A commenter on Winaero claims it affected a system-restore feature, which another commenter doubted: unverified. Missing value = Windows' own delay (about 10 s according to Make Tech Easier).",
      "source": "Winaero, Make Tech Easier and Neowin (Windows 10): create HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\Serialize, DWORD StartupDelayInMSec = 0; Windows 10 'has a 10-second delay during boot' by default (Make Tech Easier); takes effect after restart/sign-in. Microsoft Q&A answer 7617892 (Windows 11 23H2) shows StartupDelayInMSec and WaitForIdleState being used there but gives no Microsoft documentation. No Microsoft page documents the value.",
      "sources": [
        "https://winaero.com/speed-up-the-startup-of-desktop-apps-in-windows-10/",
        "https://maketecheasier.com/disable-startup-delay-in-windows",
        "https://www.neowin.net/news/registry-tweak-enables-faster-start-up-for-apps-in-windows-10/",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/a/7617892"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\Serialize",
          "name": "StartupDelayInMSec",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
